[{"input":"{#algebra-of-physical-space}\n# The Algebra of Physical Space: A Mathematical Introduction\n\nThe **algebra of physical space (APS)** {Algebra of physical space (APS)} is the real **Clifford algebra** {Clifford algebra} ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` built from ordinary three-dimensional Euclidean space. It provides a compact mathematical language for spatial geometry, rotations, relativistic spacetime, and spinors. This chapter develops the algebra and its principal structures as a foundation for the later applications in electromagnetism, elasticity, general relativity, quantum mechanics, and quantum field theory. Standard treatments include [@baylis], [@hestenes], and [@doranlasenby].\n\nThe central idea is to keep the Euclidean spatial algebra distinct from the spacetime geometry it can represent. The spatial basis vectors satisfy ``e_j^2=+1``; the Minkowski metric appears on the scalar-plus-vector subspace through **{paravector}** conjugation. We proceed from the paravector norm and causal classification to the Pauli-matrix realization, then develop multivectors {multivector} and their involutions, the quaternionic even subalgebra, six-vectors, Lorentz transformations, and spinors.\n\nPrerequisites are vector dot and cross products, complex matrices, and elementary special relativity. The goal is not to survey all applications, but to establish a consistent mathematical toolkit for them.\n\n## The geometric algebra of three-dimensional space\n\nLet the orthonormal spatial basis vectors be ``e_1,e_2,e_3``. Their geometric product is associative and satisfies\n\n```math\ne_j e_k + e_k e_j = 2\\delta_{jk},\n\\qquad e_j^2=1.\n```\n\nDifferent basis vectors anticommute. Products such as ``e_1e_2`` are **bivectors** {bivector}, representing oriented planes rather than spatial vectors. The real Clifford algebra ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` has eight basis elements:\n\n```math\n1,\\quad e_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad e_2e_3,e_3e_1,e_1e_2,\\quad I=e_1e_2e_3.\n```\n\nThe **pseudoscalar** {pseudoscalar} ``I`` obeys ``I^2=-1`` and commutes with every element of this algebra. It therefore supplies a geometric imaginary unit. In particular,\n\n```math\ne_2e_3=I e_1,\\qquad e_3e_1=I e_2,\\qquad e_1e_2=I e_3.\n```\n\nFor spatial vectors ``\\mathbf a`` and ``\\mathbf b``, the geometric product combines their dot product and oriented plane:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b).\n```\n\nHere the **wedge product** {wedge product} is the antisymmetric part of the geometric product:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a).\n```\n\nIt is a bivector recording the oriented plane and area spanned by the two vectors. The identity ``\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)`` relates this plane to its normal vector. The cross-product formula assumes the right-handed orientation specified by ``I=e_1e_2e_3``. Unlike the cross product alone, the geometric product is associative.","output":"","result":"{#algebra-of-physical-space}\n# The Algebra of Physical Space: A Mathematical Introduction\n\nThe **algebra of physical space (APS)** {Algebra of physical space (APS)} is the real **Clifford algebra** {Clifford algebra} ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` built from ordinary three-dimensional Euclidean space. It provides a compact mathematical language for spatial geometry, rotations, relativistic spacetime, and spinors. This chapter develops the algebra and its principal structures as a foundation for the later applications in electromagnetism, elasticity, general relativity, quantum mechanics, and quantum field theory. Standard treatments include [@baylis], [@hestenes], and [@doranlasenby].\n\nThe central idea is to keep the Euclidean spatial algebra distinct from the spacetime geometry it can represent. The spatial basis vectors satisfy ``e_j^2=+1``; the Minkowski metric appears on the scalar-plus-vector subspace through **{paravector}** conjugation. We proceed from the paravector norm and causal classification to the Pauli-matrix realization, then develop multivectors {multivector} and their involutions, the quaternionic even subalgebra, six-vectors, Lorentz transformations, and spinors.\n\nPrerequisites are vector dot and cross products, complex matrices, and elementary special relativity. The goal is not to survey all applications, but to establish a consistent mathematical toolkit for them.\n\n## The geometric algebra of three-dimensional space\n\nLet the orthonormal spatial basis vectors be ``e_1,e_2,e_3``. Their geometric product is associative and satisfies\n\n```math\ne_j e_k + e_k e_j = 2\\delta_{jk},\n\\qquad e_j^2=1.\n```\n\nDifferent basis vectors anticommute. Products such as ``e_1e_2`` are **bivectors** {bivector}, representing oriented planes rather than spatial vectors. The real Clifford algebra ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` has eight basis elements:\n\n```math\n1,\\quad e_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad e_2e_3,e_3e_1,e_1e_2,\\quad I=e_1e_2e_3.\n```\n\nThe **pseudoscalar** {pseudoscalar} ``I`` obeys ``I^2=-1`` and commutes with every element of this algebra. It therefore supplies a geometric imaginary unit. In particular,\n\n```math\ne_2e_3=I e_1,\\qquad e_3e_1=I e_2,\\qquad e_1e_2=I e_3.\n```\n\nFor spatial vectors ``\\mathbf a`` and ``\\mathbf b``, the geometric product combines their dot product and oriented plane:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b).\n```\n\nHere the **wedge product** {wedge product} is the antisymmetric part of the geometric product:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a).\n```\n\nIt is a bivector recording the oriented plane and area spanned by the two vectors. The identity ``\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)`` relates this plane to its normal vector. The cross-product formula assumes the right-handed orientation specified by ``I=e_1e_2e_3``. Unlike the cross product alone, the geometric product is associative.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-algebra-of-physical-space\">\n<h1>The Algebra of Physical Space: A Mathematical Introduction</h1>\n<p>The <strong>algebra of physical space (APS)</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-1\">Algebra of physical space (APS)</span> is the real <strong>Clifford algebra</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-2\">Clifford algebra</span> <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span> built from ordinary three-dimensional Euclidean space. It provides a compact mathematical language for spatial geometry, rotations, relativistic spacetime, and spinors. This chapter develops the algebra and its principal structures as a foundation for the later applications in electromagnetism, elasticity, general relativity, quantum mechanics, and quantum field theory. Standard treatments include <a href=\"#nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-baylis\" data-type=\"biblioref\">baylis</a>, <a href=\"#nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-hestenes\" data-type=\"biblioref\">hestenes</a>, and <a href=\"#nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-doranlasenby\" data-type=\"biblioref\">doranlasenby</a>.</p>\n<p>The central idea is to keep the Euclidean spatial algebra distinct from the spacetime geometry it can represent. The spatial basis vectors satisfy <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_j^2=+1\\)</span>; the Minkowski metric appears on the scalar-plus-vector subspace through <strong><span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-3\">paravector</span></strong> conjugation. We proceed from the paravector norm and causal classification to the Pauli-matrix realization, then develop multivectors <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-4\">multivector</span> and their involutions, the quaternionic even subalgebra, six-vectors, Lorentz transformations, and spinors.</p>\n<p>Prerequisites are vector dot and cross products, complex matrices, and elementary special relativity. The goal is not to survey all applications, but to establish a consistent mathematical toolkit for them.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-the-geometric-algebra-of-threedimensional-space\">\n<h1>The geometric algebra of three-dimensional space</h1>\n<p>Let the orthonormal spatial basis vectors be <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1,e_2,e_3\\)</span>. Their geometric product is associative and satisfies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\ne_j e_k + e_k e_j = 2\\delta_{jk},\n\\qquad e_j^2=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Different basis vectors anticommute. Products such as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1e_2\\)</span> are <strong>bivectors</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-5\">bivector</span>, representing oriented planes rather than spatial vectors. The real Clifford algebra <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span> has eight basis elements:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n1,\\quad e_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad e_2e_3,e_3e_1,e_1e_2,\\quad I=e_1e_2e_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The <strong>pseudoscalar</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-6\">pseudoscalar</span> <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> obeys <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I^2=-1\\)</span> and commutes with every element of this algebra. It therefore supplies a geometric imaginary unit. In particular,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\ne_2e_3=I e_1,\\qquad e_3e_1=I e_2,\\qquad e_1e_2=I e_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For spatial vectors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b\\)</span>, the geometric product combines their dot product and oriented plane:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf a\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\n=\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here the <strong>wedge product</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5050-idx-7\">wedge product</span> is the antisymmetric part of the geometric product:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\frac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It is a bivector recording the oriented plane and area spanned by the two vectors. The identity <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=I(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\)</span> relates this plane to its normal vector. The cross-product formula assumes the right-handed orientation specified by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I=e_1e_2e_3\\)</span>. Unlike the cross product alone, the geometric product is associative.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The natural complex basis\n\nBecause the pseudoscalar ``I`` is central and satisfies ``I^2=-1``, the scalar–pseudoscalar plane forms a copy of the complex numbers inside APS:\n\n```math\n\\mathbb C_I=\\{a+Ib:a,b\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n```\n\nOver this central field, a natural basis for the entire algebra is\n\n```math\n\\{1,e_1,e_2,e_3\\}.\n```\n\nThus APS has dimension eight over the reals but dimension four over ``\\mathbb C_I``. This does **not** introduce an additional imaginary unit or enlarge the algebra to its complexification: the complex structure is already supplied by its real pseudoscalar.\n\n### From four real grades to a complex scalar and vector\n\nWrite a generic multivector, with all eight coefficients real, as\n\n```math\nA=a+v_1e_1+v_2e_2+v_3e_3\n+b_1e_2e_3+b_2e_3e_1+b_3e_1e_2+Ib.\n```\n\nUsing the duality identities for bivectors, collect the terms into\n\n```math\nA=(a+Ib)+\\sum_{j=1}^3(v_j+Ib_j)e_j\n=z+\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nz=a+Ib,\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nHere ``\\mathbf b=b_1e_1+b_2e_2+b_3e_3`` is the spatial dual of the bivector part: the bivector itself is ``I\\mathbf b``. The term **complex vector** means a linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in ``\\mathbb C_I``; it need not be a pure grade-one element of the real Clifford algebra.\n\nThe grade content is therefore transparent:\n\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``z`` encode the scalar and pseudoscalar grades.\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``\\mathbf w`` encode the vector and bivector grades.\n- Real paravectors are the special case ``b=0`` and ``\\mathbf b=0``.\n\nFor example,\n\n```math\nA=2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\n=(2+5I)+(3+4I)e_1-e_2-2Ie_3.\n```\n\n### Multiplication in complex scalar–vector form\n\nExtend the spatial dot and cross products complex-bilinearly. For ``A=z+\\mathbf w`` and ``C=s+\\mathbf u``, the geometric product becomes\n\n```math\nAC=(zs+\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u)\n+\\left(z\\mathbf u+s\\mathbf w+I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u\\right).\n```\n\nThere is **no complex conjugation** in this dot product: ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\sum_j w_j u_j``. It is not the positive Hermitian inner product used for complex state vectors.\n\n### Involutions and the general inverse\n\nClifford conjugation, denoted by an overbar, reverses products and sends each spatial basis vector to its negative. Reversion, denoted by a dagger, reverses products but fixes each spatial basis vector:\n\n```math\n\\overline{AC}=\\bar C\\bar A,\\qquad \\bar e_j=-e_j,\n\\qquad\n(AC)^\\dagger=C^\\dagger A^\\dagger,\\qquad e_j^\\dagger=e_j.\n```\n\nBoth operations are real-linear and fix real scalars. Their actions on the real grades are\n\n{.wide}\n| Operation | Scalar | Vector | Bivector | Pseudoscalar |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clifford conjugation | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed | unchanged |\n| Reversion | unchanged | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed |\n\nIn particular, Clifford conjugation fixes ``I``, whereas reversion sends it to ``-I``. Writing a star for coefficientwise conjugation in ``\\mathbb C_I``, their complex scalar–vector forms are\n\n```math\n\\bar A=z-\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nA^\\dagger=z^*+\\mathbf w^*.\n```\n\nClifford conjugation is complex-linear but reverses products; reversion is complex-antilinear and also reverses products. Neither operation means simply taking the real part. On matrices, reversion is represented by Hermitian conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger.\n```\n\nThe product with the Clifford conjugate is central:\n\n```math\nA\\bar A=\\bar A A=z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n```\n\nConsequently, a generic element is invertible exactly when this complex scalar is nonzero, in which case\n\n```math\nA^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar A}{z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w}.\n```\n\nFor real paravectors this specializes to the Minkowski-norm inverse already derived.\n\n### Connection to the Pauli encoding\n\nUnder the Pauli representation introduced above, ``I`` maps to the ordinary ``i``. If ``z=a+Ib`` maps to ``\\zeta=a+ib`` and each ``w_j=v_j+Ib_j`` maps to ``\\omega_j=v_j+ib_j``, then\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\zeta\\mathbf 1_2+\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\zeta+\\omega_3&\\omega_1-i\\omega_2\\\\\n\\omega_1+i\\omega_2&\\zeta-\\omega_3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe identity and three Pauli matrices form a basis of all complex two-by-two matrices over the complex numbers. Their coefficients are\n\n```math\n\\zeta=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\omega_j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\sigma_j\\bigr).\n```\n\nFor the example above, this gives\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\begin{pmatrix}2+3i&3+5i\\\\3+3i&2+7i\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nA general APS element therefore corresponds to an arbitrary complex matrix, whereas a real paravector corresponds to a Hermitian matrix. Clifford conjugation corresponds to the adjugate, not to entrywise complex conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\det\\rho(A)=\\zeta^2-\\sum_j\\omega_j^2.\n```\n\nFor a generic multivector this determinant can be complex. On the real paravector subspace, it is precisely the real Minkowski norm. Other multivectors can also have real determinants, so reality of the determinant alone does not characterize paravectors.","output":"","result":"## The natural complex basis\n\nBecause the pseudoscalar ``I`` is central and satisfies ``I^2=-1``, the scalar–pseudoscalar plane forms a copy of the complex numbers inside APS:\n\n```math\n\\mathbb C_I=\\{a+Ib:a,b\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n```\n\nOver this central field, a natural basis for the entire algebra is\n\n```math\n\\{1,e_1,e_2,e_3\\}.\n```\n\nThus APS has dimension eight over the reals but dimension four over ``\\mathbb C_I``. This does **not** introduce an additional imaginary unit or enlarge the algebra to its complexification: the complex structure is already supplied by its real pseudoscalar.\n\n### From four real grades to a complex scalar and vector\n\nWrite a generic multivector, with all eight coefficients real, as\n\n```math\nA=a+v_1e_1+v_2e_2+v_3e_3\n+b_1e_2e_3+b_2e_3e_1+b_3e_1e_2+Ib.\n```\n\nUsing the duality identities for bivectors, collect the terms into\n\n```math\nA=(a+Ib)+\\sum_{j=1}^3(v_j+Ib_j)e_j\n=z+\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nz=a+Ib,\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nHere ``\\mathbf b=b_1e_1+b_2e_2+b_3e_3`` is the spatial dual of the bivector part: the bivector itself is ``I\\mathbf b``. The term **complex vector** means a linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in ``\\mathbb C_I``; it need not be a pure grade-one element of the real Clifford algebra.\n\nThe grade content is therefore transparent:\n\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``z`` encode the scalar and pseudoscalar grades.\n- The real and imaginary parts of ``\\mathbf w`` encode the vector and bivector grades.\n- Real paravectors are the special case ``b=0`` and ``\\mathbf b=0``.\n\nFor example,\n\n```math\nA=2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\n=(2+5I)+(3+4I)e_1-e_2-2Ie_3.\n```\n\n### Multiplication in complex scalar–vector form\n\nExtend the spatial dot and cross products complex-bilinearly. For ``A=z+\\mathbf w`` and ``C=s+\\mathbf u``, the geometric product becomes\n\n```math\nAC=(zs+\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u)\n+\\left(z\\mathbf u+s\\mathbf w+I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u\\right).\n```\n\nThere is **no complex conjugation** in this dot product: ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\sum_j w_j u_j``. It is not the positive Hermitian inner product used for complex state vectors.\n\n### Involutions and the general inverse\n\nClifford conjugation, denoted by an overbar, reverses products and sends each spatial basis vector to its negative. Reversion, denoted by a dagger, reverses products but fixes each spatial basis vector:\n\n```math\n\\overline{AC}=\\bar C\\bar A,\\qquad \\bar e_j=-e_j,\n\\qquad\n(AC)^\\dagger=C^\\dagger A^\\dagger,\\qquad e_j^\\dagger=e_j.\n```\n\nBoth operations are real-linear and fix real scalars. Their actions on the real grades are\n\n{.wide}\n| Operation | Scalar | Vector | Bivector | Pseudoscalar |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Clifford conjugation | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed | unchanged |\n| Reversion | unchanged | unchanged | sign reversed | sign reversed |\n\nIn particular, Clifford conjugation fixes ``I``, whereas reversion sends it to ``-I``. Writing a star for coefficientwise conjugation in ``\\mathbb C_I``, their complex scalar–vector forms are\n\n```math\n\\bar A=z-\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nA^\\dagger=z^*+\\mathbf w^*.\n```\n\nClifford conjugation is complex-linear but reverses products; reversion is complex-antilinear and also reverses products. Neither operation means simply taking the real part. On matrices, reversion is represented by Hermitian conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger.\n```\n\nThe product with the Clifford conjugate is central:\n\n```math\nA\\bar A=\\bar A A=z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n```\n\nConsequently, a generic element is invertible exactly when this complex scalar is nonzero, in which case\n\n```math\nA^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar A}{z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w}.\n```\n\nFor real paravectors this specializes to the Minkowski-norm inverse already derived.\n\n### Connection to the Pauli encoding\n\nUnder the Pauli representation introduced above, ``I`` maps to the ordinary ``i``. If ``z=a+Ib`` maps to ``\\zeta=a+ib`` and each ``w_j=v_j+Ib_j`` maps to ``\\omega_j=v_j+ib_j``, then\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\zeta\\mathbf 1_2+\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\zeta+\\omega_3&\\omega_1-i\\omega_2\\\\\n\\omega_1+i\\omega_2&\\zeta-\\omega_3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe identity and three Pauli matrices form a basis of all complex two-by-two matrices over the complex numbers. Their coefficients are\n\n```math\n\\zeta=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\omega_j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\sigma_j\\bigr).\n```\n\nFor the example above, this gives\n\n```math\n\\rho(A)=\\begin{pmatrix}2+3i&3+5i\\\\3+3i&2+7i\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nA general APS element therefore corresponds to an arbitrary complex matrix, whereas a real paravector corresponds to a Hermitian matrix. Clifford conjugation corresponds to the adjugate, not to entrywise complex conjugation:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\det\\rho(A)=\\zeta^2-\\sum_j\\omega_j^2.\n```\n\nFor a generic multivector this determinant can be complex. On the real paravector subspace, it is precisely the real Minkowski norm. Other multivectors can also have real determinants, so reality of the determinant alone does not characterize paravectors.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5053-the-natural-complex-basis\">\n<h1>The natural complex basis</h1>\n<p>Because the pseudoscalar <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> is central and satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I^2=-1\\)</span>, the scalar–pseudoscalar plane forms a copy of the complex numbers inside APS:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbb C_I=\\{a+Ib:a,b\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Over this central field, a natural basis for the entire algebra is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\{1,e_1,e_2,e_3\\}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus APS has dimension eight over the reals but dimension four over <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>. This does <strong>not</strong> introduce an additional imaginary unit or enlarge the algebra to its complexification: the complex structure is already supplied by its real pseudoscalar.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5053-from-four-real-grades-to-a-complex-scalar-and-vector\">\n<h1>From four real grades to a complex scalar and vector</h1>\n<p>Write a generic multivector, with all eight coefficients real, as</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=a+v_1e_1+v_2e_2+v_3e_3\n+b_1e_2e_3+b_2e_3e_1+b_3e_1e_2+Ib.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Using the duality identities for bivectors, collect the terms into</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=(a+Ib)+\\sum_{j=1}^3(v_j+Ib_j)e_j\n=z+\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nz=a+Ib,\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b=b_1e_1+b_2e_2+b_3e_3\\)</span> is the spatial dual of the bivector part: the bivector itself is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\mathbf b\\)</span>. The term <strong>complex vector</strong> means a linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>; it need not be a pure grade-one element of the real Clifford algebra.</p>\n<p>The grade content is therefore transparent:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The real and imaginary parts of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(z\\)</span> encode the scalar and pseudoscalar grades.</li>\n<li>The real and imaginary parts of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\)</span> encode the vector and bivector grades.</li>\n<li>Real paravectors are the special case <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(b=0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b=0\\)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\n=(2+5I)+(3+4I)e_1-e_2-2Ie_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5053-multiplication-in-complex-scalarvector-form\">\n<h1>Multiplication in complex scalar–vector form</h1>\n<p>Extend the spatial dot and cross products complex-bilinearly. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C=s+\\mathbf u\\)</span>, the geometric product becomes</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nAC=(zs+\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u)\n+\\left(z\\mathbf u+s\\mathbf w+I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u\\right).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>There is <strong>no complex conjugation</strong> in this dot product: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf u=\\sum_j w_j u_j\\)</span>. It is not the positive Hermitian inner product used for complex state vectors.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5053-involutions-and-the-general-inverse\">\n<h1>Involutions and the general inverse</h1>\n<p>Clifford conjugation, denoted by an overbar, reverses products and sends each spatial basis vector to its negative. Reversion, denoted by a dagger, reverses products but fixes each spatial basis vector:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\overline{AC}=\\bar C\\bar A,\\qquad \\bar e_j=-e_j,\n\\qquad\n(AC)^\\dagger=C^\\dagger A^\\dagger,\\qquad e_j^\\dagger=e_j.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Both operations are real-linear and fix real scalars. Their actions on the real grades are</p>\n<figure data-type=\"table\" class=\"wide\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Scalar</th>\n<th>Vector</th>\n<th>Bivector</th>\n<th>Pseudoscalar</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clifford conjugation</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversion</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n<td>unchanged</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n<td>sign reversed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n<p>In particular, Clifford conjugation fixes <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span>, whereas reversion sends it to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-I\\)</span>. Writing a star for coefficientwise conjugation in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>, their complex scalar–vector forms are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\bar A=z-\\mathbf w,\n\\qquad\nA^\\dagger=z^*+\\mathbf w^*.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Clifford conjugation is complex-linear but reverses products; reversion is complex-antilinear and also reverses products. Neither operation means simply taking the real part. On matrices, reversion is represented by Hermitian conjugation:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The product with the Clifford conjugate is central:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA\\bar A=\\bar A A=z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w\\in\\mathbb C_I.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Consequently, a generic element is invertible exactly when this complex scalar is nonzero, in which case</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar A}{z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For real paravectors this specializes to the Minkowski-norm inverse already derived.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5053-connection-to-the-pauli-encoding\">\n<h1>Connection to the Pauli encoding</h1>\n<p>Under the Pauli representation introduced above, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> maps to the ordinary <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(i\\)</span>. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(z=a+Ib\\)</span> maps to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\zeta=a+ib\\)</span> and each <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(w_j=v_j+Ib_j\\)</span> maps to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\omega_j=v_j+ib_j\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A)=\\zeta\\mathbf 1_2+\\sum_{j=1}^3\\omega_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\zeta+\\omega_3&amp;\\omega_1-i\\omega_2\\\\\n\\omega_1+i\\omega_2&amp;\\zeta-\\omega_3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The identity and three Pauli matrices form a basis of all complex two-by-two matrices over the complex numbers. Their coefficients are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\omega_j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\sigma_j\\bigr).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For the example above, this gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A)=\\begin{pmatrix}2+3i&amp;3+5i\\\\3+3i&amp;2+7i\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A general APS element therefore corresponds to an arbitrary complex matrix, whereas a real paravector corresponds to a Hermitian matrix. Clifford conjugation corresponds to the adjugate, not to entrywise complex conjugation:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\rho(A),\n\\qquad\n\\det\\rho(A)=\\zeta^2-\\sum_j\\omega_j^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For a generic multivector this determinant can be complex. On the real paravector subspace, it is precisely the real Minkowski norm. Other multivectors can also have real determinants, so reality of the determinant alone does not characterize paravectors.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Grade projections and the even subalgebra\n\nWrite ``\\langle A\\rangle_r`` for the grade-``r`` part of a multivector ``A``. Grades zero, one, two, and three are its scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar parts, respectively, and\n\n```math\nA=\\sum_{r=0}^{3}\\langle A\\rangle_r.\n```\n\n### Grade involution and projection formulas\n\nIn addition to reversion and Clifford conjugation, the **grade involution** {grade involution} changes the sign of odd grades:\n\n```math\n\\widehat{A_r}=(-1)^r A_r,\n\\qquad\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat A\\widehat C,\n\\qquad\n\\bar A=\\widehat{A^\\dagger}.\n```\n\nHere ``A_r`` denotes a homogeneous element of grade ``r``. It is an automorphism, whereas reversion and Clifford conjugation reverse product order. The three operations commute and square to the identity. If\n\n```math\nA=a+\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b+Ib,\n```\n\nthen their simultaneous sign patterns recover each real grade:\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_0=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A+A^\\dagger+\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_1=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A+A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n```\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_2=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A-A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_3=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A-A^\\dagger+\\bar A).\n```\n\nThese are real-linear projections. They must not be confused with projecting onto the complex scalar and complex vector, which instead gives\n\n```math\nz=\\frac12(A+\\bar A)=a+Ib,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\frac12(A-\\bar A)=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nGrade involution sends ``I`` to ``-I``. In complex scalar–vector notation it therefore acts as ``\\widehat A=z^*-\\mathbf w^*``, with the star conjugating coefficients. It is not linear over the central complex field.\n\n### Quaternions inside APS\n\nThe **even subalgebra** {even subalgebra}, consisting of scalar and bivector parts, is a real subalgebra:\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}^{+}=\\{a+I\\mathbf b:a\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\}.\n```\n\nChoose the quaternion units with the signs\n\n```math\nq_1=-Ie_1=-e_2e_3,\\qquad\nq_2=-Ie_2=-e_3e_1,\\qquad\nq_3=-Ie_3=-e_1e_2.\n```\n\nDirect multiplication gives\n\n```math\nq_jq_k=-\\delta_{jk}+\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}q_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nq_1q_2=q_3,\\qquad q_1q_2q_3=-1.\n```\n\nThus ``1,q_1,q_2,q_3`` realize the usual **quaternion** {quaternion} basis. Taking all three units as ``+Ie_j`` would reverse the sign of the cross term and would not give this multiplication convention.\n\nFor ``Q=a+\\sum_j c_jq_j``, quaternion conjugation agrees with reversion restricted to the even subalgebra:\n\n```math\nQ^\\dagger=a-\\sum_jc_jq_j,\n\\qquad\nQQ^\\dagger=a^2+\\sum_jc_j^2.\n```\n\nEvery nonzero even element is therefore invertible. This contrasts with the full APS algebra, which has zero divisors. Also, the even subalgebra is not a complex subspace over ``\\mathbb C_I``: multiplication by ``I`` changes even grades into odd ones.\n\n### Unit quaternions and rotations\n\nThe unit even elements form the group ``\\mathrm{Spin}(3)\\cong\\mathrm{SU}(2)``. A **rotor** {rotor} about the unit vector ``\\mathbf n`` has representative\n\n```math\nR=\\cos\\frac\\theta2-I\\mathbf n\\sin\\frac\\theta2,\n\\qquad RR^\\dagger=1.\n```\n\nExpanding ``\\mathbf v'=R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger`` yields Rodrigues' formula:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf v\\cos\\theta\n+(\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v)\\sin\\theta\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf v)(1-\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nBoth ``R`` and ``-R`` give the same rotation. The kernel of this action is exactly ``\\{1,-1\\}``, giving the double covering of ``\\mathrm{SO}(3)``. For example, ``R=(1-Ie_3)/\\sqrt2`` sends ``e_1`` to ``e_2``.\n\n!!! exercise \"Exercises on projections and quaternions\"\n\n    1. Apply the four grade projections to ``2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I``.\n    2. Verify ``q_2q_1=-q_3`` and derive the inverse of an arbitrary nonzero quaternion using reversion.\n    3. Derive Rodrigues' formula and check the axis component is unchanged.","output":"","result":"## Grade projections and the even subalgebra\n\nWrite ``\\langle A\\rangle_r`` for the grade-``r`` part of a multivector ``A``. Grades zero, one, two, and three are its scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar parts, respectively, and\n\n```math\nA=\\sum_{r=0}^{3}\\langle A\\rangle_r.\n```\n\n### Grade involution and projection formulas\n\nIn addition to reversion and Clifford conjugation, the **grade involution** {grade involution} changes the sign of odd grades:\n\n```math\n\\widehat{A_r}=(-1)^r A_r,\n\\qquad\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat A\\widehat C,\n\\qquad\n\\bar A=\\widehat{A^\\dagger}.\n```\n\nHere ``A_r`` denotes a homogeneous element of grade ``r``. It is an automorphism, whereas reversion and Clifford conjugation reverse product order. The three operations commute and square to the identity. If\n\n```math\nA=a+\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b+Ib,\n```\n\nthen their simultaneous sign patterns recover each real grade:\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_0=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A+A^\\dagger+\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_1=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A+A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n```\n\n```math\n\\langle A\\rangle_2=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A-A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_3=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A-A^\\dagger+\\bar A).\n```\n\nThese are real-linear projections. They must not be confused with projecting onto the complex scalar and complex vector, which instead gives\n\n```math\nz=\\frac12(A+\\bar A)=a+Ib,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\frac12(A-\\bar A)=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nGrade involution sends ``I`` to ``-I``. In complex scalar–vector notation it therefore acts as ``\\widehat A=z^*-\\mathbf w^*``, with the star conjugating coefficients. It is not linear over the central complex field.\n\n### Quaternions inside APS\n\nThe **even subalgebra** {even subalgebra}, consisting of scalar and bivector parts, is a real subalgebra:\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}^{+}=\\{a+I\\mathbf b:a\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\}.\n```\n\nChoose the quaternion units with the signs\n\n```math\nq_1=-Ie_1=-e_2e_3,\\qquad\nq_2=-Ie_2=-e_3e_1,\\qquad\nq_3=-Ie_3=-e_1e_2.\n```\n\nDirect multiplication gives\n\n```math\nq_jq_k=-\\delta_{jk}+\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}q_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nq_1q_2=q_3,\\qquad q_1q_2q_3=-1.\n```\n\nThus ``1,q_1,q_2,q_3`` realize the usual **quaternion** {quaternion} basis. Taking all three units as ``+Ie_j`` would reverse the sign of the cross term and would not give this multiplication convention.\n\nFor ``Q=a+\\sum_j c_jq_j``, quaternion conjugation agrees with reversion restricted to the even subalgebra:\n\n```math\nQ^\\dagger=a-\\sum_jc_jq_j,\n\\qquad\nQQ^\\dagger=a^2+\\sum_jc_j^2.\n```\n\nEvery nonzero even element is therefore invertible. This contrasts with the full APS algebra, which has zero divisors. Also, the even subalgebra is not a complex subspace over ``\\mathbb C_I``: multiplication by ``I`` changes even grades into odd ones.\n\n### Unit quaternions and rotations\n\nThe unit even elements form the group ``\\mathrm{Spin}(3)\\cong\\mathrm{SU}(2)``. A **rotor** {rotor} about the unit vector ``\\mathbf n`` has representative\n\n```math\nR=\\cos\\frac\\theta2-I\\mathbf n\\sin\\frac\\theta2,\n\\qquad RR^\\dagger=1.\n```\n\nExpanding ``\\mathbf v'=R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger`` yields Rodrigues' formula:\n\n```math\n\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf v\\cos\\theta\n+(\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v)\\sin\\theta\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf v)(1-\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nBoth ``R`` and ``-R`` give the same rotation. The kernel of this action is exactly ``\\{1,-1\\}``, giving the double covering of ``\\mathrm{SO}(3)``. For example, ``R=(1-Ie_3)/\\sqrt2`` sends ``e_1`` to ``e_2``.\n\n!!! exercise \"Exercises on projections and quaternions\"\n\n    1. Apply the four grade projections to ``2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I``.\n    2. Verify ``q_2q_1=-q_3`` and derive the inverse of an arbitrary nonzero quaternion using reversion.\n    3. Derive Rodrigues' formula and check the axis component is unchanged.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-grade-projections-and-the-even-subalgebra\">\n<h1>Grade projections and the even subalgebra</h1>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\langle A\\rangle_r\\)</span> for the grade-<span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> part of a multivector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span>. Grades zero, one, two, and three are its scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar parts, respectively, and</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{r=0}^{3}\\langle A\\rangle_r.\n\\]\n</div>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-grade-involution-and-projection-formulas\">\n<h1>Grade involution and projection formulas</h1>\n<p>In addition to reversion and Clifford conjugation, the <strong>grade involution</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-idx-1\">grade involution</span> changes the sign of odd grades:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\widehat{A_r}=(-1)^r A_r,\n\\qquad\n\\widehat{AC}=\\widehat A\\widehat C,\n\\qquad\n\\bar A=\\widehat{A^\\dagger}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_r\\)</span> denotes a homogeneous element of grade <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span>. It is an automorphism, whereas reversion and Clifford conjugation reverse product order. The three operations commute and square to the identity. If</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=a+\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b+Ib,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>then their simultaneous sign patterns recover each real grade:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\langle A\\rangle_0=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A+A^\\dagger+\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_1=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A+A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\]\n</div>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\langle A\\rangle_2=\\frac14(A+\\widehat A-A^\\dagger-\\bar A),\n\\qquad\n\\langle A\\rangle_3=\\frac14(A-\\widehat A-A^\\dagger+\\bar A).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>These are real-linear projections. They must not be confused with projecting onto the complex scalar and complex vector, which instead gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nz=\\frac12(A+\\bar A)=a+Ib,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf w=\\frac12(A-\\bar A)=\\mathbf v+I\\mathbf b.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Grade involution sends <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-I\\)</span>. In complex scalar–vector notation it therefore acts as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\widehat A=z^*-\\mathbf w^*\\)</span>, with the star conjugating coefficients. It is not linear over the central complex field.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-quaternions-inside-aps\">\n<h1>Quaternions inside APS</h1>\n<p>The <strong>even subalgebra</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-idx-2\">even subalgebra</span>, consisting of scalar and bivector parts, is a real subalgebra:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}^{+}=\\{a+I\\mathbf b:a\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\mathbf b\\in\\mathbb R^3\\}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Choose the quaternion units with the signs</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nq_1=-Ie_1=-e_2e_3,\\qquad\nq_2=-Ie_2=-e_3e_1,\\qquad\nq_3=-Ie_3=-e_1e_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Direct multiplication gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nq_jq_k=-\\delta_{jk}+\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}q_\\ell,\n\\qquad\nq_1q_2=q_3,\\qquad q_1q_2q_3=-1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1,q_1,q_2,q_3\\)</span> realize the usual <strong>quaternion</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-idx-3\">quaternion</span> basis. Taking all three units as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(+Ie_j\\)</span> would reverse the sign of the cross term and would not give this multiplication convention.</p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(Q=a+\\sum_j c_jq_j\\)</span>, quaternion conjugation agrees with reversion restricted to the even subalgebra:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nQ^\\dagger=a-\\sum_jc_jq_j,\n\\qquad\nQQ^\\dagger=a^2+\\sum_jc_j^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Every nonzero even element is therefore invertible. This contrasts with the full APS algebra, which has zero divisors. Also, the even subalgebra is not a complex subspace over <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>: multiplication by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span> changes even grades into odd ones.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-unit-quaternions-and-rotations\">\n<h1>Unit quaternions and rotations</h1>\n<p>The unit even elements form the group <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Spin}(3)\\cong\\mathrm{SU}(2)\\)</span>. A <strong>rotor</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5056-idx-4\">rotor</span> about the unit vector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> has representative</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nR=\\cos\\frac\\theta2-I\\mathbf n\\sin\\frac\\theta2,\n\\qquad RR^\\dagger=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Expanding <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf v'=R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger\\)</span> yields Rodrigues’ formula:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathbf v'=\\mathbf v\\cos\\theta\n+(\\mathbf n\\times\\mathbf v)\\sin\\theta\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\mathbf v)(1-\\cos\\theta).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Both <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-R\\)</span> give the same rotation. The kernel of this action is exactly <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\{1,-1\\}\\)</span>, giving the double covering of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{SO}(3)\\)</span>. For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R=(1-Ie_3)/\\sqrt2\\)</span> sends <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_2\\)</span>.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Exercises on projections and quaternions</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Apply the four grade projections to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2+3e_1-e_2+4e_2e_3-2e_1e_2+5I\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(q_2q_1=-q_3\\)</span> and derive the inverse of an arbitrary nonzero quaternion using reversion.</li>\n<li>Derive Rodrigues’ formula and check the axis component is unchanged.</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"{#pauli-representation}\n## Encoding the algebra with Pauli matrices\n\nThe **Pauli matrices** {Pauli matrices} are\n\n```math\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThey satisfy exactly the spatial Clifford relations:\n\n```math\n\\sigma_j\\sigma_k\n=\\delta_{jk}\\mathbf 1_2+i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}\\sigma_\\ell.\n```\n\nConsequently, the assignment ``e_j\\mapsto\\sigma_j`` extends to an **algebra isomorphism** {algebra isomorphism}\n\n```math\n\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\longrightarrow M_2(\\mathbb C),\n\\qquad \\rho(AB)=\\rho(A)\\rho(B).\n```\n\nHere both sides are viewed as real algebras of dimension eight. Crucially, the geometric pseudoscalar is represented by the ordinary matrix imaginary unit:\n\n```math\n\\rho(I)=\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\nFor example, ``e_1e_2=I e_3`` becomes ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3``. The matrices are therefore not merely a mnemonic for vector components: they represent the full geometric product.\n\nA paravector becomes the matrix\n\n```math\nP=\\rho(p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\np^0+p^3 & p^1-i p^2\\\\\np^1+i p^2 & p^0-p^3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nFor real components, ``P`` is **Hermitian** {Hermitian matrix}: ``P^\\dagger=P``. Conversely, every Hermitian two-by-two matrix has exactly this form. Thus real paravectors correspond precisely to Hermitian two-by-two matrices. This concrete map is the **Pauli representation** {Pauli representation} of APS.\n\nThe components are recovered without ambiguity by traces:\n\n```math\np^0=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}P,\n\\qquad\np^j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(P\\sigma_j).\n```\n\nFor arbitrary spatial vectors, the matrix counterpart of the geometric-product identity is\n\n```math\n(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf 1_2\n+i(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```","output":"","result":"{#pauli-representation}\n## Encoding the algebra with Pauli matrices\n\nThe **Pauli matrices** {Pauli matrices} are\n\n```math\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThey satisfy exactly the spatial Clifford relations:\n\n```math\n\\sigma_j\\sigma_k\n=\\delta_{jk}\\mathbf 1_2+i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}\\sigma_\\ell.\n```\n\nConsequently, the assignment ``e_j\\mapsto\\sigma_j`` extends to an **algebra isomorphism** {algebra isomorphism}\n\n```math\n\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\longrightarrow M_2(\\mathbb C),\n\\qquad \\rho(AB)=\\rho(A)\\rho(B).\n```\n\nHere both sides are viewed as real algebras of dimension eight. Crucially, the geometric pseudoscalar is represented by the ordinary matrix imaginary unit:\n\n```math\n\\rho(I)=\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\nFor example, ``e_1e_2=I e_3`` becomes ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3``. The matrices are therefore not merely a mnemonic for vector components: they represent the full geometric product.\n\nA paravector becomes the matrix\n\n```math\nP=\\rho(p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\np^0+p^3 & p^1-i p^2\\\\\np^1+i p^2 & p^0-p^3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nFor real components, ``P`` is **Hermitian** {Hermitian matrix}: ``P^\\dagger=P``. Conversely, every Hermitian two-by-two matrix has exactly this form. Thus real paravectors correspond precisely to Hermitian two-by-two matrices. This concrete map is the **Pauli representation** {Pauli representation} of APS.\n\nThe components are recovered without ambiguity by traces:\n\n```math\np^0=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}P,\n\\qquad\np^j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(P\\sigma_j).\n```\n\nFor arbitrary spatial vectors, the matrix counterpart of the geometric-product identity is\n\n```math\n(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf 1_2\n+i(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5052-pauli-representation\">\n<h1>Encoding the algebra with Pauli matrices</h1>\n<p>The <strong>Pauli matrices</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5052-idx-1\">Pauli matrices</span> are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;-i\\\\i&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>They satisfy exactly the spatial Clifford relations:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\sigma_j\\sigma_k\n=\\delta_{jk}\\mathbf 1_2+i\\sum_{\\ell=1}^3\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}\\sigma_\\ell.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Consequently, the assignment <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_j\\mapsto\\sigma_j\\)</span> extends to an <strong>algebra isomorphism</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5052-idx-2\">algebra isomorphism</span></p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\longrightarrow M_2(\\mathbb C),\n\\qquad \\rho(AB)=\\rho(A)\\rho(B).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here both sides are viewed as real algebras of dimension eight. Crucially, the geometric pseudoscalar is represented by the ordinary matrix imaginary unit:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(I)=\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\sigma_3=i\\mathbf 1_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1e_2=I e_3\\)</span> becomes <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2=i\\sigma_3\\)</span>. The matrices are therefore not merely a mnemonic for vector components: they represent the full geometric product.</p>\n<p>A paravector becomes the matrix</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP=\\rho(p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\begin{pmatrix}\np^0+p^3 &amp; p^1-i p^2\\\\\np^1+i p^2 &amp; p^0-p^3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For real components, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\)</span> is <strong>Hermitian</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5052-idx-3\">Hermitian matrix</span>: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P^\\dagger=P\\)</span>. Conversely, every Hermitian two-by-two matrix has exactly this form. Thus real paravectors correspond precisely to Hermitian two-by-two matrices. This concrete map is the <strong>Pauli representation</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5052-idx-4\">Pauli representation</span> of APS.</p>\n<p>The components are recovered without ambiguity by traces:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np^0=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}P,\n\\qquad\np^j=\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}(P\\sigma_j).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For arbitrary spatial vectors, the matrix counterpart of the geometric-product identity is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)(\\mathbf b\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)\n=(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b)\\mathbf 1_2\n+i(\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b)\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"### Terminology: reversion, Hermitian adjoint, and adjugate\n\nThe abstract Clifford-algebra operations and their matrix realizations have related names. In the Pauli representation used throughout this chapter, for **every** APS element ``A``,\n\n```math\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\bigr).\n```\n\nThus **reversion is also called Hermitian conjugation in APS**, because its matrix realization is the Hermitian adjoint (complex-conjugate transpose). **Clifford conjugation is realized by the adjugate** of the two-by-two matrix; Clifford conjugation remains the standard abstract name.\n\nFor a general complex matrix, the distinction is explicit:\n\n```math\nM=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nM^\\dagger=\\begin{pmatrix}a^*&c^*\\\\b^*&d^*\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{adj}M=\\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nHere a star denotes ordinary complex conjugation. The adjugate does **not** conjugate the entries. It is defined even when the matrix is singular and satisfies\n\n```math\nM\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)\\mathbf 1_2,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)M^{-1}\\quad\\text{if }\\det M\\ne0.\n```\n\nFor example, ``A=I+e_1`` has ``A^\\dagger=-I+e_1`` but ``\\bar A=I-e_1``. For a real paravector ``p``, instead, ``p^\\dagger=p`` while ``\\bar p`` reverses the spatial part.\n\n**Adjoint and adjugate are different operations.** These correspondences refer specifically to APS and its chosen Pauli representation. In another matrix basis the abstract reversion may require a transformed adjoint operation, and Clifford conjugation should not be identified with the adjugate for arbitrary Clifford algebras or arbitrary matrix sizes.","output":"","result":"### Terminology: reversion, Hermitian adjoint, and adjugate\n\nThe abstract Clifford-algebra operations and their matrix realizations have related names. In the Pauli representation used throughout this chapter, for **every** APS element ``A``,\n\n```math\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\bigr).\n```\n\nThus **reversion is also called Hermitian conjugation in APS**, because its matrix realization is the Hermitian adjoint (complex-conjugate transpose). **Clifford conjugation is realized by the adjugate** of the two-by-two matrix; Clifford conjugation remains the standard abstract name.\n\nFor a general complex matrix, the distinction is explicit:\n\n```math\nM=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nM^\\dagger=\\begin{pmatrix}a^*&c^*\\\\b^*&d^*\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{adj}M=\\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\\\-c&a\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nHere a star denotes ordinary complex conjugation. The adjugate does **not** conjugate the entries. It is defined even when the matrix is singular and satisfies\n\n```math\nM\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)\\mathbf 1_2,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)M^{-1}\\quad\\text{if }\\det M\\ne0.\n```\n\nFor example, ``A=I+e_1`` has ``A^\\dagger=-I+e_1`` but ``\\bar A=I-e_1``. For a real paravector ``p``, instead, ``p^\\dagger=p`` while ``\\bar p`` reverses the spatial part.\n\n**Adjoint and adjugate are different operations.** These correspondences refer specifically to APS and its chosen Pauli representation. In another matrix basis the abstract reversion may require a transformed adjoint operation, and Clifford conjugation should not be identified with the adjugate for arbitrary Clifford algebras or arbitrary matrix sizes.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5054-terminology-reversion-hermitian-adjoint-and-adjugate\">\n<h1>Terminology: reversion, Hermitian adjoint, and adjugate</h1>\n<p>The abstract Clifford-algebra operations and their matrix realizations have related names. In the Pauli representation used throughout this chapter, for <strong>every</strong> APS element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span>,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(A^\\dagger)=\\rho(A)^\\dagger,\n\\qquad\n\\rho(\\bar A)=\\operatorname{adj}\\bigl(\\rho(A)\\bigr).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus <strong>reversion is also called Hermitian conjugation in APS</strong>, because its matrix realization is the Hermitian adjoint (complex-conjugate transpose). <strong>Clifford conjugation is realized by the adjugate</strong> of the two-by-two matrix; Clifford conjugation remains the standard abstract name.</p>\n<p>For a general complex matrix, the distinction is explicit:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nM=\\begin{pmatrix}a&amp;b\\\\c&amp;d\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nM^\\dagger=\\begin{pmatrix}a^*&amp;c^*\\\\b^*&amp;d^*\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{adj}M=\\begin{pmatrix}d&amp;-b\\\\-c&amp;a\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here a star denotes ordinary complex conjugation. The adjugate does <strong>not</strong> conjugate the entries. It is defined even when the matrix is singular and satisfies</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nM\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)\\mathbf 1_2,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)M^{-1}\\quad\\text{if }\\det M\\ne0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=I+e_1\\)</span> has <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A^\\dagger=-I+e_1\\)</span> but <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar A=I-e_1\\)</span>. For a real paravector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p\\)</span>, instead, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p^\\dagger=p\\)</span> while <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar p\\)</span> reverses the spatial part.</p>\n<p><strong>Adjoint and adjugate are different operations.</strong> These correspondences refer specifically to APS and its chosen Pauli representation. In another matrix basis the abstract reversion may require a transformed adjoint operation, and Clifford conjugation should not be identified with the adjugate for arbitrary Clifford algebras or arbitrary matrix sizes.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"{#spatial-paravectors}\n## Paravectors and their Minkowski norm\n\nA real paravector is an element of the scalar-plus-vector subspace:\n\n```math\np=p^0+\\mathbf p\n=p^0+p^1e_1+p^2e_2+p^3e_3,\n\\qquad p^\\mu\\in\\mathbb R.\n```\n\nThis is a four-dimensional real subspace of the eight-dimensional algebra. For a spacetime event, take ``x=ct+\\mathbf x`` so that all components have units of length. For four-momentum, take ``p=E/c+\\mathbf p``.\n\nDefine **Clifford conjugation** {Clifford conjugation} by reversing the order of factors and changing the sign of each spatial vector. On a paravector it acts as\n\n```math\n\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p.\n```\n\nSince ``\\mathbf p^2=|\\mathbf p|^2``, the product with the conjugate is a real scalar:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\bar p p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThis is the **Minkowski norm** {Minkowski norm} with signature ``(+,-,-,-)``. The minus signs arise from conjugation, not from making the spatial basis vectors square to minus one.\n\nThe associated bilinear inner product is\n\n```math\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_M\n=\\frac12\\left(p\\bar q+q\\bar p\\right)\n=p^0q^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf q.\n```\n\nA nonzero paravector is **timelike** {timelike}, **null** {null}, or **spacelike** {spacelike} when its squared norm is positive, zero, or negative, respectively; this classification is its **causal type** {causal type}. If the norm is nonzero, its algebraic inverse is\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar p}{p\\bar p}.\n```\n\nNonzero null paravectors have no inverse. Their failure to be invertible will become especially transparent in the matrix representation.\n\n!!! note \"Paravectors do not form a subalgebra\"\n\n    Adding paravectors gives another paravector, but multiplying them generally produces a bivector term. Computations therefore take place in the full spatial Clifford algebra, even when the quantities being represented are paravectors.","output":"","result":"{#spatial-paravectors}\n## Paravectors and their Minkowski norm\n\nA real paravector is an element of the scalar-plus-vector subspace:\n\n```math\np=p^0+\\mathbf p\n=p^0+p^1e_1+p^2e_2+p^3e_3,\n\\qquad p^\\mu\\in\\mathbb R.\n```\n\nThis is a four-dimensional real subspace of the eight-dimensional algebra. For a spacetime event, take ``x=ct+\\mathbf x`` so that all components have units of length. For four-momentum, take ``p=E/c+\\mathbf p``.\n\nDefine **Clifford conjugation** {Clifford conjugation} by reversing the order of factors and changing the sign of each spatial vector. On a paravector it acts as\n\n```math\n\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p.\n```\n\nSince ``\\mathbf p^2=|\\mathbf p|^2``, the product with the conjugate is a real scalar:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\bar p p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThis is the **Minkowski norm** {Minkowski norm} with signature ``(+,-,-,-)``. The minus signs arise from conjugation, not from making the spatial basis vectors square to minus one.\n\nThe associated bilinear inner product is\n\n```math\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_M\n=\\frac12\\left(p\\bar q+q\\bar p\\right)\n=p^0q^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf q.\n```\n\nA nonzero paravector is **timelike** {timelike}, **null** {null}, or **spacelike** {spacelike} when its squared norm is positive, zero, or negative, respectively; this classification is its **causal type** {causal type}. If the norm is nonzero, its algebraic inverse is\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar p}{p\\bar p}.\n```\n\nNonzero null paravectors have no inverse. Their failure to be invertible will become especially transparent in the matrix representation.\n\n!!! note \"Paravectors do not form a subalgebra\"\n\n    Adding paravectors gives another paravector, but multiplying them generally produces a bivector term. Computations therefore take place in the full spatial Clifford algebra, even when the quantities being represented are paravectors.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-spatial-paravectors\">\n<h1>Paravectors and their Minkowski norm</h1>\n<p>A real paravector is an element of the scalar-plus-vector subspace:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=p^0+\\mathbf p\n=p^0+p^1e_1+p^2e_2+p^3e_3,\n\\qquad p^\\mu\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is a four-dimensional real subspace of the eight-dimensional algebra. For a spacetime event, take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=ct+\\mathbf x\\)</span> so that all components have units of length. For four-momentum, take <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=E/c+\\mathbf p\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Define <strong>Clifford conjugation</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-idx-1\">Clifford conjugation</span> by reversing the order of factors and changing the sign of each spatial vector. On a paravector it acts as</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\)</span>, the product with the conjugate is a real scalar:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np\\bar p=\\bar p p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is the <strong>Minkowski norm</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-idx-2\">Minkowski norm</span> with signature <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((+,-,-,-)\\)</span>. The minus signs arise from conjugation, not from making the spatial basis vectors square to minus one.</p>\n<p>The associated bilinear inner product is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\langle p,q\\rangle_M\n=\\frac12\\left(p\\bar q+q\\bar p\\right)\n=p^0q^0-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf q.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A nonzero paravector is <strong>timelike</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-idx-3\">timelike</span>, <strong>null</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-idx-4\">null</span>, or <strong>spacelike</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-idx-5\">spacelike</span> when its squared norm is positive, zero, or negative, respectively; this classification is its <strong>causal type</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5051-idx-6\">causal type</span>. If the norm is nonzero, its algebraic inverse is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar p}{p\\bar p}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Nonzero null paravectors have no inverse. Their failure to be invertible will become especially transparent in the matrix representation.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"note\">\n<h5>Paravectors do not form a subalgebra</h5>\n<p>Adding paravectors gives another paravector, but multiplying them generally produces a bivector term. Computations therefore take place in the full spatial Clifford algebra, even when the quantities being represented are paravectors.</p>\n</aside>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Determinants, conjugation, and a worked example\n\nThe Pauli encoding turns the Minkowski norm into a determinant:\n\n```math\n\\det P=(p^0+p^3)(p^0-p^3)-(p^1-i p^2)(p^1+i p^2)\n=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThe conjugate paravector is represented by the adjugate matrix:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\operatorname{adj}P,\n\\qquad\nP\\operatorname{adj}P=(\\det P)\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\n**Do not confuse Clifford conjugation with Hermitian conjugation.** Hermitian conjugation leaves a real paravector matrix unchanged, whereas Clifford conjugation reverses its spatial part. More generally, reversion in APS reverses the order of geometric factors and is represented by Hermitian conjugation. Clifford conjugation is a different anti-involution.\n\nConsider the dimensionless example\n\n```math\np=5+e_1+2e_2+3e_3,\n\\qquad\nP=\\begin{pmatrix}8&1-2i\\\\1+2i&2\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThen\n\n```math\np\\bar p=25-1-4-9=11,\n\\qquad\n\\det P=16-(1-2i)(1+2i)=11.\n```\n\nBoth descriptions give the same inverse:\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{5-e_1-2e_2-3e_3}{11},\n\\qquad\nP^{-1}=\\frac1{11}\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1+2i\\\\-1-2i&8\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe eigenvalues of any real paravector matrix are\n\n```math\n\\lambda_\\pm=p^0\\pm|\\mathbf p|.\n```\n\nHence future-directed timelike paravectors correspond to positive-definite Hermitian matrices. Future-directed nonzero null paravectors correspond to rank-one positive-semidefinite matrices. For instance,\n\n```math\nk=1+e_3\n\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\det K=0.\n```\n\nSuch a matrix can be factored as ``K=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger``, here with ``\\psi=(\\sqrt{2},0)^T``. This is one entry point to the relation between null directions and two-component spinors.","output":"","result":"## Determinants, conjugation, and a worked example\n\nThe Pauli encoding turns the Minkowski norm into a determinant:\n\n```math\n\\det P=(p^0+p^3)(p^0-p^3)-(p^1-i p^2)(p^1+i p^2)\n=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nThe conjugate paravector is represented by the adjugate matrix:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\bar p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\operatorname{adj}P,\n\\qquad\nP\\operatorname{adj}P=(\\det P)\\mathbf 1_2.\n```\n\n**Do not confuse Clifford conjugation with Hermitian conjugation.** Hermitian conjugation leaves a real paravector matrix unchanged, whereas Clifford conjugation reverses its spatial part. More generally, reversion in APS reverses the order of geometric factors and is represented by Hermitian conjugation. Clifford conjugation is a different anti-involution.\n\nConsider the dimensionless example\n\n```math\np=5+e_1+2e_2+3e_3,\n\\qquad\nP=\\begin{pmatrix}8&1-2i\\\\1+2i&2\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThen\n\n```math\np\\bar p=25-1-4-9=11,\n\\qquad\n\\det P=16-(1-2i)(1+2i)=11.\n```\n\nBoth descriptions give the same inverse:\n\n```math\np^{-1}=\\frac{5-e_1-2e_2-3e_3}{11},\n\\qquad\nP^{-1}=\\frac1{11}\\begin{pmatrix}2&-1+2i\\\\-1-2i&8\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThe eigenvalues of any real paravector matrix are\n\n```math\n\\lambda_\\pm=p^0\\pm|\\mathbf p|.\n```\n\nHence future-directed timelike paravectors correspond to positive-definite Hermitian matrices. Future-directed nonzero null paravectors correspond to rank-one positive-semidefinite matrices. For instance,\n\n```math\nk=1+e_3\n\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\det K=0.\n```\n\nSuch a matrix can be factored as ``K=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger``, here with ``\\psi=(\\sqrt{2},0)^T``. This is one entry point to the relation between null directions and two-component spinors.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5057-determinants-conjugation-and-a-worked-example\">\n<h1>Determinants, conjugation, and a worked example</h1>\n<p>The Pauli encoding turns the Minkowski norm into a determinant:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\det P=(p^0+p^3)(p^0-p^3)-(p^1-i p^2)(p^1+i p^2)\n=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The conjugate paravector is represented by the adjugate matrix:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(\\bar p)=p^0\\mathbf 1_2-\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\n=\\operatorname{adj}P,\n\\qquad\nP\\operatorname{adj}P=(\\det P)\\mathbf 1_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse Clifford conjugation with Hermitian conjugation.</strong> Hermitian conjugation leaves a real paravector matrix unchanged, whereas Clifford conjugation reverses its spatial part. More generally, reversion in APS reverses the order of geometric factors and is represented by Hermitian conjugation. Clifford conjugation is a different anti-involution.</p>\n<p>Consider the dimensionless example</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=5+e_1+2e_2+3e_3,\n\\qquad\nP=\\begin{pmatrix}8&amp;1-2i\\\\1+2i&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np\\bar p=25-1-4-9=11,\n\\qquad\n\\det P=16-(1-2i)(1+2i)=11.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Both descriptions give the same inverse:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np^{-1}=\\frac{5-e_1-2e_2-3e_3}{11},\n\\qquad\nP^{-1}=\\frac1{11}\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;-1+2i\\\\-1-2i&amp;8\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The eigenvalues of any real paravector matrix are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\pm=p^0\\pm|\\mathbf p|.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Hence future-directed timelike paravectors correspond to positive-definite Hermitian matrices. Future-directed nonzero null paravectors correspond to rank-one positive-semidefinite matrices. For instance,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nk=1+e_3\n\\quad\\longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\det K=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Such a matrix can be factored as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\\)</span>, here with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(\\sqrt{2},0)^T\\)</span>. This is one entry point to the relation between null directions and two-component spinors.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Singular elements, idempotents, and spectra\n\nA nonzero APS element that annihilates another nonzero element is a **zero divisor** {zero divisor}. Under the Pauli representation, this is exactly a singular complex matrix; for two-by-two matrices, every nonzero singular element has rank one. For ``A=z+\\mathbf w``, singularity is equivalent to\n\n```math\nz^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0.\n```\n\nA **null paravector** is a special case: its Minkowski norm vanishes. A singular general multivector need not be a paravector.\n\n### Idempotents and spinor ideals\n\nAn **idempotent** {idempotent} satisfies ``f^2=f``. For a real unit spatial vector ``\\mathbf n``,\n\n```math\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm\\mathbf n}{2}\n```\n\nare orthogonal idempotents: ``f_\\pm^2=f_\\pm``, ``f_+f_-=0``, and ``f_++f_-=1``. Their Pauli matrices are Hermitian rank-one projectors. For ``\\mathbf n=e_3``, right multiplication by ``f_\\pm`` splits the algebra into left ideals ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f_+`` and ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f_-``. Each is stable under left multiplication by APS; these ideals provide the algebraic setting for the spinors developed below.\n\n### Spectral decomposition of paravectors\n\nFor ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` with ``r=|\\mathbf p|>0``, let ``\\mathbf n=\\mathbf p/r``. Then\n\n```math\np=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-.\n```\n\nThis is the algebraic form of the Hermitian matrix spectral theorem. The eigenvalues are ``p^0\\pm r``. For example,\n\n```math\np=3+2e_1,\n\\qquad\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm e_1}{2},\n\\qquad p=5f_++f_-.\n```\n\nWhen ``\\mathbf p=0``, the matrix is scalar and both eigenvalues coincide. A future-directed timelike paravector has two positive eigenvalues, so its matrix can be written as a sum of two spinor outer products.\n\n### General APS spectra\n\nFor ``A=z+\\mathbf w``, the characteristic identity is\n\n```math\nA^2-2zA+(z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w)1=0.\n```\n\nIf ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0`` and ``\\mathbf w\\ne0``, then ``\\mathbf w`` is a nonzero **nilpotent** {nilpotent}: ``\\mathbf w^2=0``. The matrix has a repeated eigenvalue and is not diagonalizable. For example,\n\n```math\nN=e_1+Ie_2,\n\\qquad N^2=0,\n\\qquad \\rho(N)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nBy contrast, ``1+N`` is invertible, with inverse ``1-N``. Singularity, nilpotence, and idempotence are distinct properties.\n\n!!! exercise \"Exercises on singular elements and spectra\"\n\n    1. Verify ``(1+e_3)(1-e_3)=0`` and explain why neither factor can be inverted.\n    2. Find the spectral projectors of ``3+e_1+2e_2+2e_3``. Check that its eigenvalues are ``6`` and ``0``.\n    3. Show that ``(1+N)^{-1}=1-N`` and determine its determinant and repeated eigenvalue.\n    4. Construct a non-Hermitian idempotent from ``\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}`` and recover its complex scalar–vector coefficients.","output":"","result":"## Singular elements, idempotents, and spectra\n\nA nonzero APS element that annihilates another nonzero element is a **zero divisor** {zero divisor}. Under the Pauli representation, this is exactly a singular complex matrix; for two-by-two matrices, every nonzero singular element has rank one. For ``A=z+\\mathbf w``, singularity is equivalent to\n\n```math\nz^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0.\n```\n\nA **null paravector** is a special case: its Minkowski norm vanishes. A singular general multivector need not be a paravector.\n\n### Idempotents and spinor ideals\n\nAn **idempotent** {idempotent} satisfies ``f^2=f``. For a real unit spatial vector ``\\mathbf n``,\n\n```math\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm\\mathbf n}{2}\n```\n\nare orthogonal idempotents: ``f_\\pm^2=f_\\pm``, ``f_+f_-=0``, and ``f_++f_-=1``. Their Pauli matrices are Hermitian rank-one projectors. For ``\\mathbf n=e_3``, right multiplication by ``f_\\pm`` splits the algebra into left ideals ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f_+`` and ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f_-``. Each is stable under left multiplication by APS; these ideals provide the algebraic setting for the spinors developed below.\n\n### Spectral decomposition of paravectors\n\nFor ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` with ``r=|\\mathbf p|>0``, let ``\\mathbf n=\\mathbf p/r``. Then\n\n```math\np=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-.\n```\n\nThis is the algebraic form of the Hermitian matrix spectral theorem. The eigenvalues are ``p^0\\pm r``. For example,\n\n```math\np=3+2e_1,\n\\qquad\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm e_1}{2},\n\\qquad p=5f_++f_-.\n```\n\nWhen ``\\mathbf p=0``, the matrix is scalar and both eigenvalues coincide. A future-directed timelike paravector has two positive eigenvalues, so its matrix can be written as a sum of two spinor outer products.\n\n### General APS spectra\n\nFor ``A=z+\\mathbf w``, the characteristic identity is\n\n```math\nA^2-2zA+(z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w)1=0.\n```\n\nIf ``\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0`` and ``\\mathbf w\\ne0``, then ``\\mathbf w`` is a nonzero **nilpotent** {nilpotent}: ``\\mathbf w^2=0``. The matrix has a repeated eigenvalue and is not diagonalizable. For example,\n\n```math\nN=e_1+Ie_2,\n\\qquad N^2=0,\n\\qquad \\rho(N)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nBy contrast, ``1+N`` is invertible, with inverse ``1-N``. Singularity, nilpotence, and idempotence are distinct properties.\n\n!!! exercise \"Exercises on singular elements and spectra\"\n\n    1. Verify ``(1+e_3)(1-e_3)=0`` and explain why neither factor can be inverted.\n    2. Find the spectral projectors of ``3+e_1+2e_2+2e_3``. Check that its eigenvalues are ``6`` and ``0``.\n    3. Show that ``(1+N)^{-1}=1-N`` and determine its determinant and repeated eigenvalue.\n    4. Construct a non-Hermitian idempotent from ``\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}`` and recover its complex scalar–vector coefficients.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-singular-elements-idempotents-and-spectra\">\n<h1>Singular elements, idempotents, and spectra</h1>\n<p>A nonzero APS element that annihilates another nonzero element is a <strong>zero divisor</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-idx-1\">zero divisor</span>. Under the Pauli representation, this is exactly a singular complex matrix; for two-by-two matrices, every nonzero singular element has rank one. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span>, singularity is equivalent to</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nz^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A <strong>null paravector</strong> is a special case: its Minkowski norm vanishes. A singular general multivector need not be a paravector.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-idempotents-and-spinor-ideals\">\n<h1>Idempotents and spinor ideals</h1>\n<p>An <strong>idempotent</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-idx-2\">idempotent</span> satisfies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^2=f\\)</span>. For a real unit spatial vector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span>,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm\\mathbf n}{2}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>are orthogonal idempotents: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f_\\pm^2=f_\\pm\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f_+f_-=0\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f_++f_-=1\\)</span>. Their Pauli matrices are Hermitian rank-one projectors. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=e_3\\)</span>, right multiplication by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f_\\pm\\)</span> splits the algebra into left ideals <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f_+\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f_-\\)</span>. Each is stable under left multiplication by APS; these ideals provide the algebraic setting for the spinors developed below.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-spectral-decomposition-of-paravectors\">\n<h1>Spectral decomposition of paravectors</h1>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=p^0+\\mathbf p\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r=|\\mathbf p|&gt;0\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=\\mathbf p/r\\)</span>. Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is the algebraic form of the Hermitian matrix spectral theorem. The eigenvalues are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p^0\\pm r\\)</span>. For example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np=3+2e_1,\n\\qquad\nf_\\pm=\\frac{1\\pm e_1}{2},\n\\qquad p=5f_++f_-.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>When <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p=0\\)</span>, the matrix is scalar and both eigenvalues coincide. A future-directed timelike paravector has two positive eigenvalues, so its matrix can be written as a sum of two spinor outer products.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-general-aps-spectra\">\n<h1>General APS spectra</h1>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A=z+\\mathbf w\\)</span>, the characteristic identity is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA^2-2zA+(z^2-\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w)1=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf w=0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\ne0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w\\)</span> is a nonzero <strong>nilpotent</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5058-idx-3\">nilpotent</span>: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf w^2=0\\)</span>. The matrix has a repeated eigenvalue and is not diagonalizable. For example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nN=e_1+Ie_2,\n\\qquad N^2=0,\n\\qquad \\rho(N)=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;2\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>By contrast, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1+N\\)</span> is invertible, with inverse <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1-N\\)</span>. Singularity, nilpotence, and idempotence are distinct properties.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Exercises on singular elements and spectra</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1+e_3)(1-e_3)=0\\)</span> and explain why neither factor can be inverted.</li>\n<li>Find the spectral projectors of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(3+e_1+2e_2+2e_3\\)</span>. Check that its eigenvalues are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(6\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(0\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1+N)^{-1}=1-N\\)</span> and determine its determinant and repeated eigenvalue.</li>\n<li>Construct a non-Hermitian idempotent from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\)</span> and recover its complex scalar–vector coefficients.</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Real six-vectors as complex spatial vectors\n\nThe complex-vector part of an APS element has a second useful interpretation: it is a **real six-vector** {six-vector}. Explicitly,\n\n```math\nF=\\mathbf a+I\\mathbf b\n=\\sum_{j=1}^3(a_j+Ib_j)e_j,\n\\qquad a_j,b_j\\in\\mathbb R.\n```\n\nIts six real coordinates are the three vector coefficients and the three bivector coefficients. A real basis is\n\n```math\ne_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad Ie_1,Ie_2,Ie_3.\n```\n\nThus the six-vector space is exactly the complex-vector space already introduced, viewed over the reals. The number six describes its dimension, **not its Clifford grade**: APS has no grade-six elements. A six-vector generally contains grades one and two, but neither a scalar nor a pseudoscalar. Equivalently, ``\\bar F=-F``.\n\n### Pauli encoding and quadratic invariants\n\nPut ``f_j=a_j+ib_j``. The matrix encoding is traceless:\n\n```math\nM=\\rho(F)=\\sum_j f_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}f_3&f_1-if_2\\\\f_1+if_2&-f_3\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}M=0.\n```\n\nEvery traceless complex two-by-two matrix represents a unique real six-vector. Unlike a real paravector matrix, it need not be Hermitian; it is Hermitian exactly when ``\\mathbf b=0``.\n\nUsing the complex-bilinear dot product, the square is central:\n\n```math\nF^2=|\\mathbf a|^2-|\\mathbf b|^2+2I(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b).\n```\n\nThe cross terms combine into a dot product because ``\\mathbf a\\mathbf b+\\mathbf b\\mathbf a=2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b``. If the central scalar ``F^2`` is represented by the complex number ``s``, then\n\n```math\nM^2=s\\mathbf 1_2,\\qquad \\det M=-s.\n```\n\nThere are therefore two real quadratic invariants: ``|\\mathbf a|^2-|\\mathbf b|^2`` and ``\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b``. In particular, a nonzero six-vector has square zero exactly when its two real parts have equal lengths and are perpendicular. For example, ``e_1+Ie_2`` is nilpotent, as in the later spectral discussion. This is different from a null real paravector, whose square need not vanish.\n\n### Similarity action versus the paravector sandwich\n\nThe Lorentz spin transformations developed later act differently on the two spaces. If ``\\rho(\\ell)=L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)``, use\n\n```math\np'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger\n\\quad\\text{for paravectors},\n\\qquad\nF'=\\ell F\\ell^{-1}\n\\quad\\text{for six-vectors}.\n```\n\nIn matrices the second rule is ``M'=LML^{-1}``. Similarity preserves trace, so it preserves the six-vector space. Since ``F^2`` is central,\n\n```math\n(F')^2=\\ell F^2\\ell^{-1}=F^2.\n```\n\nThis proves invariance of both real quadratic quantities above. Under spatial rotations the vector and bivector parts rotate separately; under boosts they generally mix. Neither part alone is an invariant subspace of the full Lorentz action.\n\nFor example, take the boost representative\n\n```math\n\\ell=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2+e_3\\sinh\\frac\\eta2,\n\\qquad\n\\ell^{-1}=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2-e_3\\sinh\\frac\\eta2.\n```\n\nFor the six-vector ``F=e_1``, multiplication gives\n\n```math\nF'=\\ell e_1\\ell^{-1}\n=\\cosh\\eta\\,e_1+I\\sinh\\eta\\,e_2.\n```\n\nIts square remains ``\\cosh^2\\eta-\\sinh^2\\eta=1``. By contrast, treating the same initial algebra element ``e_1`` as a paravector and applying the paravector sandwich gives ``\\ell e_1\\ell^\\dagger=e_1``. The role and transformation law of an element matter, not just its initial coordinates.\n\n### Biparavectors: the geometric origin of six components\n\nFor real paravectors ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` and ``q=q^0+\\mathbf q``, define the antisymmetric product\n\n```math\n\\mathcal B(p,q)=\\frac12(p\\bar q-q\\bar p)\n=q^0\\mathbf p-p^0\\mathbf q-I(\\mathbf p\\times\\mathbf q).\n```\n\nIt is a six-vector, called a **biparavector** {biparavector}. In the exterior algebra of the four-dimensional real paravector space, the corresponding object is a spacetime bivector. This is not the ordinary spatial wedge product of two grade-one spatial vectors: conjugation in the definition is essential.\n\nThe six independent antisymmetric pairs of four basis paravectors span the six-vector space. For example,\n\n```math\n\\mathcal B(e_j,1)=e_j,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal B(e_j,e_k)=-e_je_k\\quad(j\\ne k).\n```\n\nA single ``\\mathcal B(p,q)`` represents a simple spacetime bivector, that is, one antisymmetric pair. General six-vectors are sums of such terms; they need not be simple. In this convention simplicity is equivalent to ``\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=0``, or to the absence of a pseudoscalar part in ``F^2``.\n\nTo check covariance, determinant one implies ``\\bar\\ell=\\ell^{-1}`` and\n\n```math\n\\overline{q'}=(\\ell^\\dagger)^{-1}\\bar q\\ell^{-1}.\n```\n\nConsequently ``p'\\overline{q'}=\\ell(p\\bar q)\\ell^{-1}``, and antisymmetrizing proves\n\n```math\n\\mathcal B(p',q')=\\ell\\mathcal B(p,q)\\ell^{-1}.\n```\n\nThis derives the six-vector similarity law from the paravector sandwich rather than assuming it independently.\n\n!!! exercise \"Six-vector exercises\"\n\n    1. Encode ``F=e_1+2Ie_3`` as a matrix and verify ``F^2=-3`` and ``\\det\\rho(F)=3``.\n    2. Compute ``\\mathcal B(1,e_2)`` and ``\\mathcal B(e_1,e_2)`` and compare their spatial grades.\n    3. Verify the boost example directly using the diagonal matrix ``L=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})``.\n    4. Show that ``e_1+Ie_1`` is not a simple biparavector by evaluating its square.","output":"","result":"## Real six-vectors as complex spatial vectors\n\nThe complex-vector part of an APS element has a second useful interpretation: it is a **real six-vector** {six-vector}. Explicitly,\n\n```math\nF=\\mathbf a+I\\mathbf b\n=\\sum_{j=1}^3(a_j+Ib_j)e_j,\n\\qquad a_j,b_j\\in\\mathbb R.\n```\n\nIts six real coordinates are the three vector coefficients and the three bivector coefficients. A real basis is\n\n```math\ne_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad Ie_1,Ie_2,Ie_3.\n```\n\nThus the six-vector space is exactly the complex-vector space already introduced, viewed over the reals. The number six describes its dimension, **not its Clifford grade**: APS has no grade-six elements. A six-vector generally contains grades one and two, but neither a scalar nor a pseudoscalar. Equivalently, ``\\bar F=-F``.\n\n### Pauli encoding and quadratic invariants\n\nPut ``f_j=a_j+ib_j``. The matrix encoding is traceless:\n\n```math\nM=\\rho(F)=\\sum_j f_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}f_3&f_1-if_2\\\\f_1+if_2&-f_3\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}M=0.\n```\n\nEvery traceless complex two-by-two matrix represents a unique real six-vector. Unlike a real paravector matrix, it need not be Hermitian; it is Hermitian exactly when ``\\mathbf b=0``.\n\nUsing the complex-bilinear dot product, the square is central:\n\n```math\nF^2=|\\mathbf a|^2-|\\mathbf b|^2+2I(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b).\n```\n\nThe cross terms combine into a dot product because ``\\mathbf a\\mathbf b+\\mathbf b\\mathbf a=2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b``. If the central scalar ``F^2`` is represented by the complex number ``s``, then\n\n```math\nM^2=s\\mathbf 1_2,\\qquad \\det M=-s.\n```\n\nThere are therefore two real quadratic invariants: ``|\\mathbf a|^2-|\\mathbf b|^2`` and ``\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b``. In particular, a nonzero six-vector has square zero exactly when its two real parts have equal lengths and are perpendicular. For example, ``e_1+Ie_2`` is nilpotent, as in the later spectral discussion. This is different from a null real paravector, whose square need not vanish.\n\n### Similarity action versus the paravector sandwich\n\nThe Lorentz spin transformations developed later act differently on the two spaces. If ``\\rho(\\ell)=L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)``, use\n\n```math\np'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger\n\\quad\\text{for paravectors},\n\\qquad\nF'=\\ell F\\ell^{-1}\n\\quad\\text{for six-vectors}.\n```\n\nIn matrices the second rule is ``M'=LML^{-1}``. Similarity preserves trace, so it preserves the six-vector space. Since ``F^2`` is central,\n\n```math\n(F')^2=\\ell F^2\\ell^{-1}=F^2.\n```\n\nThis proves invariance of both real quadratic quantities above. Under spatial rotations the vector and bivector parts rotate separately; under boosts they generally mix. Neither part alone is an invariant subspace of the full Lorentz action.\n\nFor example, take the boost representative\n\n```math\n\\ell=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2+e_3\\sinh\\frac\\eta2,\n\\qquad\n\\ell^{-1}=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2-e_3\\sinh\\frac\\eta2.\n```\n\nFor the six-vector ``F=e_1``, multiplication gives\n\n```math\nF'=\\ell e_1\\ell^{-1}\n=\\cosh\\eta\\,e_1+I\\sinh\\eta\\,e_2.\n```\n\nIts square remains ``\\cosh^2\\eta-\\sinh^2\\eta=1``. By contrast, treating the same initial algebra element ``e_1`` as a paravector and applying the paravector sandwich gives ``\\ell e_1\\ell^\\dagger=e_1``. The role and transformation law of an element matter, not just its initial coordinates.\n\n### Biparavectors: the geometric origin of six components\n\nFor real paravectors ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` and ``q=q^0+\\mathbf q``, define the antisymmetric product\n\n```math\n\\mathcal B(p,q)=\\frac12(p\\bar q-q\\bar p)\n=q^0\\mathbf p-p^0\\mathbf q-I(\\mathbf p\\times\\mathbf q).\n```\n\nIt is a six-vector, called a **biparavector** {biparavector}. In the exterior algebra of the four-dimensional real paravector space, the corresponding object is a spacetime bivector. This is not the ordinary spatial wedge product of two grade-one spatial vectors: conjugation in the definition is essential.\n\nThe six independent antisymmetric pairs of four basis paravectors span the six-vector space. For example,\n\n```math\n\\mathcal B(e_j,1)=e_j,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal B(e_j,e_k)=-e_je_k\\quad(j\\ne k).\n```\n\nA single ``\\mathcal B(p,q)`` represents a simple spacetime bivector, that is, one antisymmetric pair. General six-vectors are sums of such terms; they need not be simple. In this convention simplicity is equivalent to ``\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=0``, or to the absence of a pseudoscalar part in ``F^2``.\n\nTo check covariance, determinant one implies ``\\bar\\ell=\\ell^{-1}`` and\n\n```math\n\\overline{q'}=(\\ell^\\dagger)^{-1}\\bar q\\ell^{-1}.\n```\n\nConsequently ``p'\\overline{q'}=\\ell(p\\bar q)\\ell^{-1}``, and antisymmetrizing proves\n\n```math\n\\mathcal B(p',q')=\\ell\\mathcal B(p,q)\\ell^{-1}.\n```\n\nThis derives the six-vector similarity law from the paravector sandwich rather than assuming it independently.\n\n!!! exercise \"Six-vector exercises\"\n\n    1. Encode ``F=e_1+2Ie_3`` as a matrix and verify ``F^2=-3`` and ``\\det\\rho(F)=3``.\n    2. Compute ``\\mathcal B(1,e_2)`` and ``\\mathcal B(e_1,e_2)`` and compare their spatial grades.\n    3. Verify the boost example directly using the diagonal matrix ``L=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})``.\n    4. Show that ``e_1+Ie_1`` is not a simple biparavector by evaluating its square.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5055-real-sixvectors-as-complex-spatial-vectors\">\n<h1>Real six-vectors as complex spatial vectors</h1>\n<p>The complex-vector part of an APS element has a second useful interpretation: it is a <strong>real six-vector</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5055-idx-1\">six-vector</span>. Explicitly,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nF=\\mathbf a+I\\mathbf b\n=\\sum_{j=1}^3(a_j+Ib_j)e_j,\n\\qquad a_j,b_j\\in\\mathbb R.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its six real coordinates are the three vector coefficients and the three bivector coefficients. A real basis is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\ne_1,e_2,e_3,\\quad Ie_1,Ie_2,Ie_3.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus the six-vector space is exactly the complex-vector space already introduced, viewed over the reals. The number six describes its dimension, <strong>not its Clifford grade</strong>: APS has no grade-six elements. A six-vector generally contains grades one and two, but neither a scalar nor a pseudoscalar. Equivalently, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar F=-F\\)</span>.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5055-pauli-encoding-and-quadratic-invariants\">\n<h1>Pauli encoding and quadratic invariants</h1>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f_j=a_j+ib_j\\)</span>. The matrix encoding is traceless:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nM=\\rho(F)=\\sum_j f_j\\sigma_j\n=\\begin{pmatrix}f_3&amp;f_1-if_2\\\\f_1+if_2&amp;-f_3\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}M=0.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Every traceless complex two-by-two matrix represents a unique real six-vector. Unlike a real paravector matrix, it need not be Hermitian; it is Hermitian exactly when <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b=0\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>Using the complex-bilinear dot product, the square is central:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nF^2=|\\mathbf a|^2-|\\mathbf b|^2+2I(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The cross terms combine into a dot product because <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b+\\mathbf b\\mathbf a=2\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b\\)</span>. If the central scalar <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F^2\\)</span> is represented by the complex number <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(s\\)</span>, then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nM^2=s\\mathbf 1_2,\\qquad \\det M=-s.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>There are therefore two real quadratic invariants: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(|\\mathbf a|^2-|\\mathbf b|^2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b\\)</span>. In particular, a nonzero six-vector has square zero exactly when its two real parts have equal lengths and are perpendicular. For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1+Ie_2\\)</span> is nilpotent, as in the later spectral discussion. This is different from a null real paravector, whose square need not vanish.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5055-similarity-action-versus-the-paravector-sandwich\">\n<h1>Similarity action versus the paravector sandwich</h1>\n<p>The Lorentz spin transformations developed later act differently on the two spaces. If <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\rho(\\ell)=L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span>, use</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger\n\\quad\\text{for paravectors},\n\\qquad\nF'=\\ell F\\ell^{-1}\n\\quad\\text{for six-vectors}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>In matrices the second rule is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M'=LML^{-1}\\)</span>. Similarity preserves trace, so it preserves the six-vector space. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F^2\\)</span> is central,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(F')^2=\\ell F^2\\ell^{-1}=F^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This proves invariance of both real quadratic quantities above. Under spatial rotations the vector and bivector parts rotate separately; under boosts they generally mix. Neither part alone is an invariant subspace of the full Lorentz action.</p>\n<p>For example, take the boost representative</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\ell=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2+e_3\\sinh\\frac\\eta2,\n\\qquad\n\\ell^{-1}=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2-e_3\\sinh\\frac\\eta2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For the six-vector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F=e_1\\)</span>, multiplication gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nF'=\\ell e_1\\ell^{-1}\n=\\cosh\\eta\\,e_1+I\\sinh\\eta\\,e_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its square remains <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\cosh^2\\eta-\\sinh^2\\eta=1\\)</span>. By contrast, treating the same initial algebra element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1\\)</span> as a paravector and applying the paravector sandwich gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ell e_1\\ell^\\dagger=e_1\\)</span>. The role and transformation law of an element matter, not just its initial coordinates.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5055-biparavectors-the-geometric-origin-of-six-components\">\n<h1>Biparavectors: the geometric origin of six components</h1>\n<p>For real paravectors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=p^0+\\mathbf p\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(q=q^0+\\mathbf q\\)</span>, define the antisymmetric product</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathcal B(p,q)=\\frac12(p\\bar q-q\\bar p)\n=q^0\\mathbf p-p^0\\mathbf q-I(\\mathbf p\\times\\mathbf q).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It is a six-vector, called a <strong>biparavector</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5055-idx-2\">biparavector</span>. In the exterior algebra of the four-dimensional real paravector space, the corresponding object is a spacetime bivector. This is not the ordinary spatial wedge product of two grade-one spatial vectors: conjugation in the definition is essential.</p>\n<p>The six independent antisymmetric pairs of four basis paravectors span the six-vector space. For example,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathcal B(e_j,1)=e_j,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal B(e_j,e_k)=-e_je_k\\quad(j\\ne k).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A single <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal B(p,q)\\)</span> represents a simple spacetime bivector, that is, one antisymmetric pair. General six-vectors are sums of such terms; they need not be simple. In this convention simplicity is equivalent to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b=0\\)</span>, or to the absence of a pseudoscalar part in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F^2\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>To check covariance, determinant one implies <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar\\ell=\\ell^{-1}\\)</span> and</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\overline{q'}=(\\ell^\\dagger)^{-1}\\bar q\\ell^{-1}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Consequently <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p'\\overline{q'}=\\ell(p\\bar q)\\ell^{-1}\\)</span>, and antisymmetrizing proves</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathcal B(p',q')=\\ell\\mathcal B(p,q)\\ell^{-1}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This derives the six-vector similarity law from the paravector sandwich rather than assuming it independently.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Six-vector exercises</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Encode <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F=e_1+2Ie_3\\)</span> as a matrix and verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F^2=-3\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\det\\rho(F)=3\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Compute <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal B(1,e_2)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal B(e_1,e_2)\\)</span> and compare their spatial grades.</li>\n<li>Verify the boost example directly using the diagonal matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_1+Ie_1\\)</span> is not a simple biparavector by evaluating its square.</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Rotations and Lorentz transformations\n\nLet ``L`` be a complex two-by-two matrix with determinant one. Transform a paravector matrix by the **sandwich action**\n\n```math\nP'=LPL^\\dagger,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis preserves Hermiticity and the determinant:\n\n```math\n(P')^\\dagger=P',\n\\qquad\n\\det P'=\\det L\\,\\det P\\,\\overline{\\det L}=\\det P.\n```\n\nIt therefore defines a real linear transformation preserving the Minkowski norm. These transformations give precisely the proper orthochronous Lorentz group: spatial orientation and time orientation are preserved. The matrices ``L`` and ``-L`` produce the same action, so this is a two-to-one covering, not a one-to-one identification. Reflections and time reversal are not included.\n\nIn APS notation the same action is ``p'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger``, where ``\\rho(\\ell)=L`` and the dagger denotes reversion. A general Lorentz transformation element is not itself restricted to the paravector subspace.\n\n### Spatial rotations\n\nFor an active right-handed rotation through angle ``\\theta`` about a unit spatial axis ``\\mathbf n``, choose\n\n```math\nU=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{i\\theta}{2}\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nSince ``U^\\dagger=U^{-1}``, the transformation ``P'=UPU^\\dagger`` leaves the scalar part fixed and rotates the spatial part. For example, rotation about the third axis sends\n\n```math\nU\\sigma_1U^\\dagger\n=\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_2.\n```\n\nIts APS counterpart is the rotor ``\\ell=\\exp(-I\\mathbf n\\theta/2)``. The half-angle explains why a full turn changes the rotor's sign while leaving the represented vector unchanged.\n\n### Boosts\n\nAn active boost of rapidity ``\\eta`` along ``\\mathbf n`` is represented, with the sign convention used here, by\n\n```math\nB=\\exp\\left(\\frac\\eta2\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nThe hyperbolic functions arise because ``(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=\\mathbf 1_2``. Unlike a rotation matrix, ``B`` is Hermitian rather than unitary. Resolve ``\\mathbf p=p_\\parallel\\mathbf n+\\mathbf p_\\perp``. The sandwich action gives\n\n```math\np'^0=p^0\\cosh\\eta+p_\\parallel\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\np'_\\parallel=p_\\parallel\\cosh\\eta+p^0\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p'_\\perp=\\mathbf p_\\perp.\n```\n\nHere ``\\beta=v/c=\\tanh\\eta`` and ``\\gamma=\\cosh\\eta``. A passive change to a frame moving in the positive axis direction uses the inverse boost, obtained by replacing ``\\eta`` with ``-\\eta``. This distinction accounts for the opposite signs often seen in textbook coordinate formulas.\n\nAs a check, boost the rest four-momentum ``P=mc\\mathbf 1_2``:\n\n```math\nP'=mc\\,BB^\\dagger\n=mc\\left(\\cosh\\eta\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\eta\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right).\n```\n\nThus ``E=\\gamma mc^2`` and ``\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v``, while ``\\det P'=m^2c^2`` remains unchanged.","output":"","result":"## Rotations and Lorentz transformations\n\nLet ``L`` be a complex two-by-two matrix with determinant one. Transform a paravector matrix by the **sandwich action**\n\n```math\nP'=LPL^\\dagger,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis preserves Hermiticity and the determinant:\n\n```math\n(P')^\\dagger=P',\n\\qquad\n\\det P'=\\det L\\,\\det P\\,\\overline{\\det L}=\\det P.\n```\n\nIt therefore defines a real linear transformation preserving the Minkowski norm. These transformations give precisely the proper orthochronous Lorentz group: spatial orientation and time orientation are preserved. The matrices ``L`` and ``-L`` produce the same action, so this is a two-to-one covering, not a one-to-one identification. Reflections and time reversal are not included.\n\nIn APS notation the same action is ``p'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger``, where ``\\rho(\\ell)=L`` and the dagger denotes reversion. A general Lorentz transformation element is not itself restricted to the paravector subspace.\n\n### Spatial rotations\n\nFor an active right-handed rotation through angle ``\\theta`` about a unit spatial axis ``\\mathbf n``, choose\n\n```math\nU=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{i\\theta}{2}\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nSince ``U^\\dagger=U^{-1}``, the transformation ``P'=UPU^\\dagger`` leaves the scalar part fixed and rotates the spatial part. For example, rotation about the third axis sends\n\n```math\nU\\sigma_1U^\\dagger\n=\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_2.\n```\n\nIts APS counterpart is the rotor ``\\ell=\\exp(-I\\mathbf n\\theta/2)``. The half-angle explains why a full turn changes the rotor's sign while leaving the represented vector unchanged.\n\n### Boosts\n\nAn active boost of rapidity ``\\eta`` along ``\\mathbf n`` is represented, with the sign convention used here, by\n\n```math\nB=\\exp\\left(\\frac\\eta2\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nThe hyperbolic functions arise because ``(\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=\\mathbf 1_2``. Unlike a rotation matrix, ``B`` is Hermitian rather than unitary. Resolve ``\\mathbf p=p_\\parallel\\mathbf n+\\mathbf p_\\perp``. The sandwich action gives\n\n```math\np'^0=p^0\\cosh\\eta+p_\\parallel\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\np'_\\parallel=p_\\parallel\\cosh\\eta+p^0\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p'_\\perp=\\mathbf p_\\perp.\n```\n\nHere ``\\beta=v/c=\\tanh\\eta`` and ``\\gamma=\\cosh\\eta``. A passive change to a frame moving in the positive axis direction uses the inverse boost, obtained by replacing ``\\eta`` with ``-\\eta``. This distinction accounts for the opposite signs often seen in textbook coordinate formulas.\n\nAs a check, boost the rest four-momentum ``P=mc\\mathbf 1_2``:\n\n```math\nP'=mc\\,BB^\\dagger\n=mc\\left(\\cosh\\eta\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\eta\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right).\n```\n\nThus ``E=\\gamma mc^2`` and ``\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v``, while ``\\det P'=m^2c^2`` remains unchanged.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5059-rotations-and-lorentz-transformations\">\n<h1>Rotations and Lorentz transformations</h1>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> be a complex two-by-two matrix with determinant one. Transform a paravector matrix by the <strong>sandwich action</strong></p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP'=LPL^\\dagger,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This preserves Hermiticity and the determinant:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(P')^\\dagger=P',\n\\qquad\n\\det P'=\\det L\\,\\det P\\,\\overline{\\det L}=\\det P.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>It therefore defines a real linear transformation preserving the Minkowski norm. These transformations give precisely the proper orthochronous Lorentz group: spatial orientation and time orientation are preserved. The matrices <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-L\\)</span> produce the same action, so this is a two-to-one covering, not a one-to-one identification. Reflections and time reversal are not included.</p>\n<p>In APS notation the same action is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p'=\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\rho(\\ell)=L\\)</span> and the dagger denotes reversion. A general Lorentz transformation element is not itself restricted to the paravector subspace.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5059-spatial-rotations\">\n<h1>Spatial rotations</h1>\n<p>For an active right-handed rotation through angle <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta\\)</span> about a unit spatial axis <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span>, choose</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU=\\exp\\left(-\\frac{i\\theta}{2}\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cos\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2-i\\sin\\frac\\theta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(U^\\dagger=U^{-1}\\)</span>, the transformation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P'=UPU^\\dagger\\)</span> leaves the scalar part fixed and rotates the spatial part. For example, rotation about the third axis sends</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU\\sigma_1U^\\dagger\n=\\cos\\theta\\,\\sigma_1+\\sin\\theta\\,\\sigma_2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its APS counterpart is the rotor <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ell=\\exp(-I\\mathbf n\\theta/2)\\)</span>. The half-angle explains why a full turn changes the rotor’s sign while leaving the represented vector unchanged.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5059-boosts\">\n<h1>Boosts</h1>\n<p>An active boost of rapidity <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\eta\\)</span> along <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span> is represented, with the sign convention used here, by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nB=\\exp\\left(\\frac\\eta2\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right)\n=\\cosh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\frac\\eta2\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The hyperbolic functions arise because <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span>. Unlike a rotation matrix, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B\\)</span> is Hermitian rather than unitary. Resolve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p=p_\\parallel\\mathbf n+\\mathbf p_\\perp\\)</span>. The sandwich action gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np'^0=p^0\\cosh\\eta+p_\\parallel\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\np'_\\parallel=p_\\parallel\\cosh\\eta+p^0\\sinh\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p'_\\perp=\\mathbf p_\\perp.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\beta=v/c=\\tanh\\eta\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\gamma=\\cosh\\eta\\)</span>. A passive change to a frame moving in the positive axis direction uses the inverse boost, obtained by replacing <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\eta\\)</span> with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-\\eta\\)</span>. This distinction accounts for the opposite signs often seen in textbook coordinate formulas.</p>\n<p>As a check, boost the rest four-momentum <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P=mc\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nP'=mc\\,BB^\\dagger\n=mc\\left(\\cosh\\eta\\,\\mathbf 1_2+\\sinh\\eta\\,\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma\\right).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(E=\\gamma mc^2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf p=\\gamma m\\mathbf v\\)</span>, while <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\det P'=m^2c^2\\)</span> remains unchanged.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Commutators and Lie algebras\n\nAn associative algebra also defines a Lie bracket through the commutator:\n\n```math\n[A,C]=AC-CA.\n```\n\nAssociativity implies the Jacobi identity. For complex scalar–vector elements the central scalar parts drop out:\n\n```math\n[z+\\mathbf w,s+\\mathbf u]=2I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u.\n```\n\nThe complex vectors therefore form a three-dimensional complex Lie algebra. Under the Pauli map they are exactly the traceless matrices, giving ``\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C)``. Viewed over the reals this algebra has dimension six. The full associative APS algebra has complex dimension four; it must not be identified with its traceless commutator subalgebra.\n\n### Generators consistent with the sandwich convention\n\nDefine real rotation and boost generators within APS by\n\n```math\nJ_j=-\\frac12Ie_j,\\qquad K_j=\\frac12e_j.\n```\n\nTheir matrices are ``-i\\sigma_j/2`` and ``\\sigma_j/2``, respectively. Their signs match the active rotations and boosts already used. Since ``[e_j,e_k]=2I\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}e_\\ell``, direct substitution gives\n\n```math\n[J_j,J_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[J_j,K_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}K_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[K_j,K_k]=-\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell.\n```\n\nFor example, ``[K_1,K_2]=Ie_3/2=-J_3``. The real span of the ``J_j`` is ``\\mathfrak{su}(2)``, the Lie algebra of unit quaternions. Adding the ``K_j`` gives the real Lie algebra of the Lorentz spin group. Boost generators alone do not form a Lie subalgebra because their commutators produce rotations.\n\n### Infinitesimal action on paravectors\n\nLet ``\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)``. Differentiating the sandwich action yields\n\n```math\n\\delta p=Gp+pG^\\dagger.\n```\n\nFor ``G=-I\\mathbf a/2``, where ``\\mathbf a`` is a real infinitesimal rotation vector, ``G^\\dagger=-G``. For ``G=\\mathbf b/2``, where ``\\mathbf b`` is a real infinitesimal boost parameter, ``G^\\dagger=G``. Hence\n\n```math\n\\delta p^0=\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf p,\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf p+p^0\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nThese equations also check the generator signs without relying on a particular matrix basis. They imply ``\\delta[(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2]=0`` directly. Note that only the rotation action reduces to a commutator with ``p``; the boost action uses an anticommutator.\n\nA small group commutator makes noncommutativity concrete:\n\n```math\n(1+sG)(1+tH)(1-sG)(1-tH)\n=1+st[G,H]+O(s^2,t^2),\n```\n\nwhere only terms through first order in each parameter are displayed. More precisely, using exact group elements and their inverses gives a leading mixed term ``st[G,H]`` with higher terms of orders ``s^2t`` and ``st^2``. Two small boosts along distinct axes therefore generate a rotation at mixed order.\n\n!!! exercise \"Lie-algebra exercises\"\n\n    1. Derive the three generator brackets and verify one Jacobi identity involving two boosts and a rotation.\n    2. Show that the infinitesimal component formulas preserve the Minkowski quadratic form.\n    3. Prove that the trace of every matrix commutator vanishes and explain why the complex-vector subspace is closed under the bracket.","output":"","result":"## Commutators and Lie algebras\n\nAn associative algebra also defines a Lie bracket through the commutator:\n\n```math\n[A,C]=AC-CA.\n```\n\nAssociativity implies the Jacobi identity. For complex scalar–vector elements the central scalar parts drop out:\n\n```math\n[z+\\mathbf w,s+\\mathbf u]=2I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u.\n```\n\nThe complex vectors therefore form a three-dimensional complex Lie algebra. Under the Pauli map they are exactly the traceless matrices, giving ``\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C)``. Viewed over the reals this algebra has dimension six. The full associative APS algebra has complex dimension four; it must not be identified with its traceless commutator subalgebra.\n\n### Generators consistent with the sandwich convention\n\nDefine real rotation and boost generators within APS by\n\n```math\nJ_j=-\\frac12Ie_j,\\qquad K_j=\\frac12e_j.\n```\n\nTheir matrices are ``-i\\sigma_j/2`` and ``\\sigma_j/2``, respectively. Their signs match the active rotations and boosts already used. Since ``[e_j,e_k]=2I\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}e_\\ell``, direct substitution gives\n\n```math\n[J_j,J_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[J_j,K_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}K_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[K_j,K_k]=-\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell.\n```\n\nFor example, ``[K_1,K_2]=Ie_3/2=-J_3``. The real span of the ``J_j`` is ``\\mathfrak{su}(2)``, the Lie algebra of unit quaternions. Adding the ``K_j`` gives the real Lie algebra of the Lorentz spin group. Boost generators alone do not form a Lie subalgebra because their commutators produce rotations.\n\n### Infinitesimal action on paravectors\n\nLet ``\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)``. Differentiating the sandwich action yields\n\n```math\n\\delta p=Gp+pG^\\dagger.\n```\n\nFor ``G=-I\\mathbf a/2``, where ``\\mathbf a`` is a real infinitesimal rotation vector, ``G^\\dagger=-G``. For ``G=\\mathbf b/2``, where ``\\mathbf b`` is a real infinitesimal boost parameter, ``G^\\dagger=G``. Hence\n\n```math\n\\delta p^0=\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf p,\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf p+p^0\\mathbf b.\n```\n\nThese equations also check the generator signs without relying on a particular matrix basis. They imply ``\\delta[(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2]=0`` directly. Note that only the rotation action reduces to a commutator with ``p``; the boost action uses an anticommutator.\n\nA small group commutator makes noncommutativity concrete:\n\n```math\n(1+sG)(1+tH)(1-sG)(1-tH)\n=1+st[G,H]+O(s^2,t^2),\n```\n\nwhere only terms through first order in each parameter are displayed. More precisely, using exact group elements and their inverses gives a leading mixed term ``st[G,H]`` with higher terms of orders ``s^2t`` and ``st^2``. Two small boosts along distinct axes therefore generate a rotation at mixed order.\n\n!!! exercise \"Lie-algebra exercises\"\n\n    1. Derive the three generator brackets and verify one Jacobi identity involving two boosts and a rotation.\n    2. Show that the infinitesimal component formulas preserve the Minkowski quadratic form.\n    3. Prove that the trace of every matrix commutator vanishes and explain why the complex-vector subspace is closed under the bracket.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5060-commutators-and-lie-algebras\">\n<h1>Commutators and Lie algebras</h1>\n<p>An associative algebra also defines a Lie bracket through the commutator:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[A,C]=AC-CA.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Associativity implies the Jacobi identity. For complex scalar–vector elements the central scalar parts drop out:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[z+\\mathbf w,s+\\mathbf u]=2I\\mathbf w\\times\\mathbf u.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The complex vectors therefore form a three-dimensional complex Lie algebra. Under the Pauli map they are exactly the traceless matrices, giving <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span>. Viewed over the reals this algebra has dimension six. The full associative APS algebra has complex dimension four; it must not be identified with its traceless commutator subalgebra.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5060-generators-consistent-with-the-sandwich-convention\">\n<h1>Generators consistent with the sandwich convention</h1>\n<p>Define real rotation and boost generators within APS by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nJ_j=-\\frac12Ie_j,\\qquad K_j=\\frac12e_j.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Their matrices are <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-i\\sigma_j/2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_j/2\\)</span>, respectively. Their signs match the active rotations and boosts already used. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\([e_j,e_k]=2I\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}e_\\ell\\)</span>, direct substitution gives</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n[J_j,J_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[J_j,K_k]=\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}K_\\ell,\n\\qquad\n[K_j,K_k]=-\\sum_\\ell\\epsilon_{jk\\ell}J_\\ell.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\([K_1,K_2]=Ie_3/2=-J_3\\)</span>. The real span of the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(J_j\\)</span> is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\)</span>, the Lie algebra of unit quaternions. Adding the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K_j\\)</span> gives the real Lie algebra of the Lorentz spin group. Boost generators alone do not form a Lie subalgebra because their commutators produce rotations.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5060-infinitesimal-action-on-paravectors\">\n<h1>Infinitesimal action on paravectors</h1>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)\\)</span>. Differentiating the sandwich action yields</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\delta p=Gp+pG^\\dagger.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G=-I\\mathbf a/2\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\)</span> is a real infinitesimal rotation vector, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G^\\dagger=-G\\)</span>. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G=\\mathbf b/2\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b\\)</span> is a real infinitesimal boost parameter, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(G^\\dagger=G\\)</span>. Hence</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\delta p^0=\\mathbf b\\cdot\\mathbf p,\n\\qquad\n\\delta\\mathbf p=\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf p+p^0\\mathbf b.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>These equations also check the generator signs without relying on a particular matrix basis. They imply <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\delta[(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2]=0\\)</span> directly. Note that only the rotation action reduces to a commutator with <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p\\)</span>; the boost action uses an anticommutator.</p>\n<p>A small group commutator makes noncommutativity concrete:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n(1+sG)(1+tH)(1-sG)(1-tH)\n=1+st[G,H]+O(s^2,t^2),\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>where only terms through first order in each parameter are displayed. More precisely, using exact group elements and their inverses gives a leading mixed term <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(st[G,H]\\)</span> with higher terms of orders <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(s^2t\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(st^2\\)</span>. Two small boosts along distinct axes therefore generate a rotation at mixed order.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Lie-algebra exercises</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Derive the three generator brackets and verify one Jacobi identity involving two boosts and a rotation.</li>\n<li>Show that the infinitesimal component formulas preserve the Minkowski quadratic form.</li>\n<li>Prove that the trace of every matrix commutator vanishes and explain why the complex-vector subspace is closed under the bracket.</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"### Six-vectors and the adjoint representation\n\nThe real span of the generators ``J_j`` and ``K_j`` is exactly the six-vector space: it is the vector-plus-bivector part of APS. Closure under the commutator makes it a Lie algebra, although it is not closed under the geometric product. A six-vector is a possible infinitesimal generator, not generally a determinant-one group element.\n\nThe similarity action ``F\\mapsto\\ell F\\ell^{-1}`` is the group's **adjoint representation** on this Lie algebra. Here *adjoint representation* means group conjugation, not Hermitian conjugation or the matrix adjugate. For ``\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)``, its derivative is\n\n```math\n\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\ell(t)F\\ell(t)^{-1}\\bigr)\\right|_{t=0}\n=[G,F].\n```\n\nUnlike the paravector action, the six-vector action is therefore a commutator for both rotation and boost generators. This links the six-vector geometry to the traceless-matrix and Lie-bracket descriptions without identifying six-vectors with spinor columns.","output":"","result":"### Six-vectors and the adjoint representation\n\nThe real span of the generators ``J_j`` and ``K_j`` is exactly the six-vector space: it is the vector-plus-bivector part of APS. Closure under the commutator makes it a Lie algebra, although it is not closed under the geometric product. A six-vector is a possible infinitesimal generator, not generally a determinant-one group element.\n\nThe similarity action ``F\\mapsto\\ell F\\ell^{-1}`` is the group's **adjoint representation** on this Lie algebra. Here *adjoint representation* means group conjugation, not Hermitian conjugation or the matrix adjugate. For ``\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)``, its derivative is\n\n```math\n\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\ell(t)F\\ell(t)^{-1}\\bigr)\\right|_{t=0}\n=[G,F].\n```\n\nUnlike the paravector action, the six-vector action is therefore a commutator for both rotation and boost generators. This links the six-vector geometry to the traceless-matrix and Lie-bracket descriptions without identifying six-vectors with spinor columns.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5061-sixvectors-and-the-adjoint-representation\">\n<h1>Six-vectors and the adjoint representation</h1>\n<p>The real span of the generators <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(J_j\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K_j\\)</span> is exactly the six-vector space: it is the vector-plus-bivector part of APS. Closure under the commutator makes it a Lie algebra, although it is not closed under the geometric product. A six-vector is a possible infinitesimal generator, not generally a determinant-one group element.</p>\n<p>The similarity action <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F\\mapsto\\ell F\\ell^{-1}\\)</span> is the group’s <strong>adjoint representation</strong> on this Lie algebra. Here <em>adjoint representation</em> means group conjugation, not Hermitian conjugation or the matrix adjugate. For <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\ell(t)=1+tG+O(t^2)\\)</span>, its derivative is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\ell(t)F\\ell(t)^{-1}\\bigr)\\right|_{t=0}\n=[G,F].\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Unlike the paravector action, the six-vector action is therefore a commutator for both rotation and boost generators. This links the six-vector geometry to the traceless-matrix and Lie-bracket descriptions without identifying six-vectors with spinor columns.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Spinors and their relation to paravectors\n\nA **spinor** {spinor} is characterized by how it transforms, not simply by having complex components. The most direct example is a two-component column\n\n```math\n\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb C^2,\n\\qquad \\psi'=L\\psi,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis is one of the chiral Weyl-spinor representations of the proper Lorentz spin group. Under spatial rotations, ``L=U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)``, it is the familiar Pauli spinor. A spinor transforms by one-sided multiplication, whereas a paravector matrix transforms by ``P'=LPL^\\dagger``. The same number of components does not make these the same kind of object: their transformation laws differ.\n\n### Rotation and the half-angle\n\nFor a rotation about the third axis,\n\n```math\nU(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}&0\\\\0&e^{i\\theta/2}\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThus ``U(2\\pi)=-\\mathbf 1_2`` while ``U(4\\pi)=\\mathbf 1_2``. A full turn changes the spinor's sign while leaving its vector bilinears unchanged. In quantum mechanics an overall phase does not change an isolated pure state, though relative phases can matter in interference.\n\n### A null paravector from a spinor\n\nThe outer product of a nonzero spinor with its Hermitian adjoint is\n\n```math\nK=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\n=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&\\alpha\\beta^*\\\\\\beta\\alpha^*&|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}\n=k^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nIts components are\n\n```math\nk^0=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2}{2},\\qquad\nk^1=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^2=\\operatorname{Im}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^3=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2-|\\beta|^2}{2}.\n```\n\nThe matrix has rank one, so ``\\det K=(k^0)^2-|\\mathbf k|^2=0`` and ``k^0>0``. It therefore represents a future-directed null paravector. Moreover,\n\n```math\nK'=(L\\psi)(L\\psi)^\\dagger=LKL^\\dagger,\n```\n\nso the spinor transformation induces the paravector sandwich law. Every future-directed nonzero null paravector factors this way, with its overall scale reflected in the spinor magnitude. A future timelike paravector instead requires a sum of two spinor outer products.\n\n### Pauli spinors and spatial directions\n\nFor a normalized spinor ``\\psi^\\dagger\\psi=1``, define ``n_j=\\psi^\\dagger\\sigma_j\\psi``. Then\n\n```math\n\\psi\\psi^\\dagger=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma),\n\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=1.\n```\n\nA representative is\n\n```math\n\\psi(\\theta,\\phi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\\\e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nFor example, ``(1,1)^T/\\sqrt2`` gives ``\\mathbf n=e_1``. For a spin-one-half pure state, ``\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=(\\hbar/2)\\mathbf n``. These expectation values use the Hermitian product, not the complex-bilinear dot product used for complex vectors in APS. Rotations preserve the normalization; boosts generally do not, though they preserve the null character of ``K``.\n\n### Spinors as minimal left ideals\n\nChoose the reference idempotent ``f=(1+e_3)/2``. Its Pauli matrix is ``\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)``. The left ideal ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f`` consists of elements\n\n```math\n\\Psi=(\\alpha+\\beta e_1)f,\n\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C_I,\n```\n\nwhose matrices have only a first column:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\Psi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&0\\\\\\beta&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThat column is exactly the two-component spinor, and left multiplication by a Lorentz spin representative reproduces ``\\psi'=L\\psi``. The ideal is minimal: it has no smaller nonzero left ideal. The choice of ``f`` selects a reference axis for the representation, not a preferred physical direction.\n\nFor rotations, normalized Pauli spinors can also be represented as ``\\Psi=Rf``, where ``R`` is a unit even element. Its matrix has the form\n\n```math\n\\rho(R)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&-\\beta^*\\\\\\beta&\\alpha^*\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2),\n\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n```\n\nThe associated direction is ``\\mathbf n=Re_3R^\\dagger``. Rotor representatives are useful for rotations, while ideal spinors make the two-component representation explicit. A two-component Weyl spinor is not itself an invertible Lorentz transformation; a Dirac spinor combines the two opposite chiral representations.\n\n!!! exercise \"Spinor exercises\"\n\n    1. Verify the four component formulas for ``K`` and check directly that its Minkowski norm vanishes.\n    2. Show that ``(1,0)^T``, ``(0,1)^T``, and ``(1,i)^T/\\sqrt2`` give the directions ``e_3``, ``-e_3``, and ``e_2``.\n    3. Apply ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to ``\\psi=(1,0)^T``. Show that ``K`` is multiplied by ``e^\\eta`` and remains null.\n    4. Verify ``f^2=f`` and ``e_3f=f``. Explain why left multiplication by any APS element preserves ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f``.","output":"","result":"## Spinors and their relation to paravectors\n\nA **spinor** {spinor} is characterized by how it transforms, not simply by having complex components. The most direct example is a two-component column\n\n```math\n\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb C^2,\n\\qquad \\psi'=L\\psi,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n```\n\nThis is one of the chiral Weyl-spinor representations of the proper Lorentz spin group. Under spatial rotations, ``L=U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)``, it is the familiar Pauli spinor. A spinor transforms by one-sided multiplication, whereas a paravector matrix transforms by ``P'=LPL^\\dagger``. The same number of components does not make these the same kind of object: their transformation laws differ.\n\n### Rotation and the half-angle\n\nFor a rotation about the third axis,\n\n```math\nU(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}&0\\\\0&e^{i\\theta/2}\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThus ``U(2\\pi)=-\\mathbf 1_2`` while ``U(4\\pi)=\\mathbf 1_2``. A full turn changes the spinor's sign while leaving its vector bilinears unchanged. In quantum mechanics an overall phase does not change an isolated pure state, though relative phases can matter in interference.\n\n### A null paravector from a spinor\n\nThe outer product of a nonzero spinor with its Hermitian adjoint is\n\n```math\nK=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\n=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&\\alpha\\beta^*\\\\\\beta\\alpha^*&|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}\n=k^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n```\n\nIts components are\n\n```math\nk^0=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2}{2},\\qquad\nk^1=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^2=\\operatorname{Im}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^3=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2-|\\beta|^2}{2}.\n```\n\nThe matrix has rank one, so ``\\det K=(k^0)^2-|\\mathbf k|^2=0`` and ``k^0>0``. It therefore represents a future-directed null paravector. Moreover,\n\n```math\nK'=(L\\psi)(L\\psi)^\\dagger=LKL^\\dagger,\n```\n\nso the spinor transformation induces the paravector sandwich law. Every future-directed nonzero null paravector factors this way, with its overall scale reflected in the spinor magnitude. A future timelike paravector instead requires a sum of two spinor outer products.\n\n### Pauli spinors and spatial directions\n\nFor a normalized spinor ``\\psi^\\dagger\\psi=1``, define ``n_j=\\psi^\\dagger\\sigma_j\\psi``. Then\n\n```math\n\\psi\\psi^\\dagger=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma),\n\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=1.\n```\n\nA representative is\n\n```math\n\\psi(\\theta,\\phi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\\\e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta).\n```\n\nFor example, ``(1,1)^T/\\sqrt2`` gives ``\\mathbf n=e_1``. For a spin-one-half pure state, ``\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=(\\hbar/2)\\mathbf n``. These expectation values use the Hermitian product, not the complex-bilinear dot product used for complex vectors in APS. Rotations preserve the normalization; boosts generally do not, though they preserve the null character of ``K``.\n\n### Spinors as minimal left ideals\n\nChoose the reference idempotent ``f=(1+e_3)/2``. Its Pauli matrix is ``\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)``. The left ideal ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f`` consists of elements\n\n```math\n\\Psi=(\\alpha+\\beta e_1)f,\n\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C_I,\n```\n\nwhose matrices have only a first column:\n\n```math\n\\rho(\\Psi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&0\\\\\\beta&0\\end{pmatrix}.\n```\n\nThat column is exactly the two-component spinor, and left multiplication by a Lorentz spin representative reproduces ``\\psi'=L\\psi``. The ideal is minimal: it has no smaller nonzero left ideal. The choice of ``f`` selects a reference axis for the representation, not a preferred physical direction.\n\nFor rotations, normalized Pauli spinors can also be represented as ``\\Psi=Rf``, where ``R`` is a unit even element. Its matrix has the form\n\n```math\n\\rho(R)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&-\\beta^*\\\\\\beta&\\alpha^*\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2),\n\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n```\n\nThe associated direction is ``\\mathbf n=Re_3R^\\dagger``. Rotor representatives are useful for rotations, while ideal spinors make the two-component representation explicit. A two-component Weyl spinor is not itself an invertible Lorentz transformation; a Dirac spinor combines the two opposite chiral representations.\n\n!!! exercise \"Spinor exercises\"\n\n    1. Verify the four component formulas for ``K`` and check directly that its Minkowski norm vanishes.\n    2. Show that ``(1,0)^T``, ``(0,1)^T``, and ``(1,i)^T/\\sqrt2`` give the directions ``e_3``, ``-e_3``, and ``e_2``.\n    3. Apply ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to ``\\psi=(1,0)^T``. Show that ``K`` is multiplied by ``e^\\eta`` and remains null.\n    4. Verify ``f^2=f`` and ``e_3f=f``. Explain why left multiplication by any APS element preserves ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5062-spinors-and-their-relation-to-paravectors\">\n<h1>Spinors and their relation to paravectors</h1>\n<p>A <strong>spinor</strong> <span data-type=\"index-term\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5062-idx-1\">spinor</span> is characterized by how it transforms, not simply by having complex components. The most direct example is a two-component column</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\psi=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb C^2,\n\\qquad \\psi'=L\\psi,\n\\qquad L\\in\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This is one of the chiral Weyl-spinor representations of the proper Lorentz spin group. Under spatial rotations, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L=U\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2)\\)</span>, it is the familiar Pauli spinor. A spinor transforms by one-sided multiplication, whereas a paravector matrix transforms by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P'=LPL^\\dagger\\)</span>. The same number of components does not make these the same kind of object: their transformation laws differ.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5062-rotation-and-the-halfangle\">\n<h1>Rotation and the half-angle</h1>\n<p>For a rotation about the third axis,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nU(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}e^{-i\\theta/2}&amp;0\\\\0&amp;e^{i\\theta/2}\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Thus <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(U(2\\pi)=-\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span> while <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(U(4\\pi)=\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span>. A full turn changes the spinor’s sign while leaving its vector bilinears unchanged. In quantum mechanics an overall phase does not change an isolated pure state, though relative phases can matter in interference.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5062-a-null-paravector-from-a-spinor\">\n<h1>A null paravector from a spinor</h1>\n<p>The outer product of a nonzero spinor with its Hermitian adjoint is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nK=\\psi\\psi^\\dagger\n=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&amp;\\alpha\\beta^*\\\\\\beta\\alpha^*&amp;|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}\n=k^0\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf k\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Its components are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nk^0=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2}{2},\\qquad\nk^1=\\operatorname{Re}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^2=\\operatorname{Im}(\\alpha^*\\beta),\\qquad\nk^3=\\frac{|\\alpha|^2-|\\beta|^2}{2}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The matrix has rank one, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\det K=(k^0)^2-|\\mathbf k|^2=0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(k^0&gt;0\\)</span>. It therefore represents a future-directed null paravector. Moreover,</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nK'=(L\\psi)(L\\psi)^\\dagger=LKL^\\dagger,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>so the spinor transformation induces the paravector sandwich law. Every future-directed nonzero null paravector factors this way, with its overall scale reflected in the spinor magnitude. A future timelike paravector instead requires a sum of two spinor outer products.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5062-pauli-spinors-and-spatial-directions\">\n<h1>Pauli spinors and spatial directions</h1>\n<p>For a normalized spinor <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi^\\dagger\\psi=1\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(n_j=\\psi^\\dagger\\sigma_j\\psi\\)</span>. Then</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\psi\\psi^\\dagger=\\frac12(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma),\n\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A representative is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\psi(\\theta,\\phi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)\\\\e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta).\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For example, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1,1)^T/\\sqrt2\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=e_1\\)</span>. For a spin-one-half pure state, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\langle\\mathbf S\\rangle=(\\hbar/2)\\mathbf n\\)</span>. These expectation values use the Hermitian product, not the complex-bilinear dot product used for complex vectors in APS. Rotations preserve the normalization; boosts generally do not, though they preserve the null character of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5062-spinors-as-minimal-left-ideals\">\n<h1>Spinors as minimal left ideals</h1>\n<p>Choose the reference idempotent <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f=(1+e_3)/2\\)</span>. Its Pauli matrix is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)\\)</span>. The left ideal <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f\\)</span> consists of elements</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\Psi=(\\alpha+\\beta e_1)f,\n\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C_I,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>whose matrices have only a first column:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(\\Psi)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&amp;0\\\\\\beta&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>That column is exactly the two-component spinor, and left multiplication by a Lorentz spin representative reproduces <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi'=L\\psi\\)</span>. The ideal is minimal: it has no smaller nonzero left ideal. The choice of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> selects a reference axis for the representation, not a preferred physical direction.</p>\n<p>For rotations, normalized Pauli spinors can also be represented as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\Psi=Rf\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(R\\)</span> is a unit even element. Its matrix has the form</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\rho(R)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha&amp;-\\beta^*\\\\\\beta&amp;\\alpha^*\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathrm{SU}(2),\n\\qquad |\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The associated direction is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n=Re_3R^\\dagger\\)</span>. Rotor representatives are useful for rotations, while ideal spinors make the two-component representation explicit. A two-component Weyl spinor is not itself an invertible Lorentz transformation; a Dirac spinor combines the two opposite chiral representations.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"exercise\">\n<h5>Spinor exercises</h5>\n<ol>\n<li>Verify the four component formulas for <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span> and check directly that its Minkowski norm vanishes.</li>\n<li>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1,0)^T\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((0,1)^T\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1,i)^T/\\sqrt2\\)</span> give the directions <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_3\\)</span>, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-e_3\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_2\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>Apply <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi=(1,0)^T\\)</span>. Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(K\\)</span> is multiplied by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e^\\eta\\)</span> and remains null.</li>\n<li>Verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^2=f\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_3f=f\\)</span>. Explain why left multiplication by any APS element preserves <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f\\)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n</aside>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"!!! note \"Connections to later physics\"\n\n    The material in this chapter is mathematical groundwork rather than a full treatment of these applications.\n\n    - **Electromagnetism and elasticity:** APS can encode fields and stresses using multivectors. Later chapters will specify the appropriate physical quantities, constitutive laws, and equations.\n    - **General relativity:** Paravectors and Lorentz transformations provide a flat-spacetime starting point; curved-spacetime applications require additional geometric structure.\n    - **Quantum mechanics:** Pauli spinors connect APS rotations with spin-½ states. The detailed geometry of spinor rays and their phase structure is deferred to a later chapter.\n\n    The purpose here is to establish common algebraic language so that each application can be developed on its own terms.","output":"","result":"!!! note \"Connections to later physics\"\n\n    The material in this chapter is mathematical groundwork rather than a full treatment of these applications.\n\n    - **Electromagnetism and elasticity:** APS can encode fields and stresses using multivectors. Later chapters will specify the appropriate physical quantities, constitutive laws, and equations.\n    - **General relativity:** Paravectors and Lorentz transformations provide a flat-spacetime starting point; curved-spacetime applications require additional geometric structure.\n    - **Quantum mechanics:** Pauli spinors connect APS rotations with spin-½ states. The detailed geometry of spinor rays and their phase structure is deferred to a later chapter.\n\n    The purpose here is to establish common algebraic language so that each application can be developed on its own terms.","resultHTML":"<aside data-type=\"note\">\n<h5>Connections to later physics</h5>\n<p>The material in this chapter is mathematical groundwork rather than a full treatment of these applications.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Electromagnetism and elasticity:</strong> APS can encode fields and stresses using multivectors. Later chapters will specify the appropriate physical quantities, constitutive laws, and equations.</li>\n<li><strong>General relativity:</strong> Paravectors and Lorentz transformations provide a flat-spacetime starting point; curved-spacetime applications require additional geometric structure.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantum mechanics:</strong> Pauli spinors connect APS rotations with spin-½ states. The detailed geometry of spinor rays and their phase structure is deferred to a later chapter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The purpose here is to establish common algebraic language so that each application can be developed on its own terms.</p>\n</aside>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Synthesis: a mathematical toolkit for physics\n\nFor an inertial observer, a four-vector splits into a temporal scalar and a spatial vector. APS encodes this observer-dependent split as a paravector. Lorentz transformations mix the two parts, but preserve their Minkowski norm. The scalar part is not an observer-independent absolute time.\n\nFor four-momentum, the invariant gives the relativistic energy–momentum relation:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\frac{E^2}{c^2}-|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^2,\n\\qquad\nE^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nFor light, ``m=0`` and the momentum matrix is singular. This is a geometric property of a null four-vector, not a defect of its representation.\n\nKeep these distinctions in view:\n\n- The spatial algebra is Euclidean: ``e_j^2=+1``. Minkowski signature belongs to the paravector norm involving Clifford conjugation.\n- A spatial vector, bivector, paravector, and six-vector are distinct algebraic types, even when represented by matrices of the same size.\n- The matrix ``i\\mathbf 1_2`` represents the spatial pseudoscalar, not a fourth spatial basis vector.\n- Hermitian conjugation and Clifford conjugation are different operations.\n- The Lorentz paravector action uses ``L^\\dagger``, not generally ``L^{-1}``.\n- The Pauli representation realizes APS as an algebra; merely arranging four numbers in a generic matrix does not preserve its multiplication.\n\nLater chapters apply this toolkit to physical fields and dynamical equations.","output":"","result":"## Synthesis: a mathematical toolkit for physics\n\nFor an inertial observer, a four-vector splits into a temporal scalar and a spatial vector. APS encodes this observer-dependent split as a paravector. Lorentz transformations mix the two parts, but preserve their Minkowski norm. The scalar part is not an observer-independent absolute time.\n\nFor four-momentum, the invariant gives the relativistic energy–momentum relation:\n\n```math\np\\bar p=\\frac{E^2}{c^2}-|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^2,\n\\qquad\nE^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2.\n```\n\nFor light, ``m=0`` and the momentum matrix is singular. This is a geometric property of a null four-vector, not a defect of its representation.\n\nKeep these distinctions in view:\n\n- The spatial algebra is Euclidean: ``e_j^2=+1``. Minkowski signature belongs to the paravector norm involving Clifford conjugation.\n- A spatial vector, bivector, paravector, and six-vector are distinct algebraic types, even when represented by matrices of the same size.\n- The matrix ``i\\mathbf 1_2`` represents the spatial pseudoscalar, not a fourth spatial basis vector.\n- Hermitian conjugation and Clifford conjugation are different operations.\n- The Lorentz paravector action uses ``L^\\dagger``, not generally ``L^{-1}``.\n- The Pauli representation realizes APS as an algebra; merely arranging four numbers in a generic matrix does not preserve its multiplication.\n\nLater chapters apply this toolkit to physical fields and dynamical equations.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5064-synthesis-a-mathematical-toolkit-for-physics\">\n<h1>Synthesis: a mathematical toolkit for physics</h1>\n<p>For an inertial observer, a four-vector splits into a temporal scalar and a spatial vector. APS encodes this observer-dependent split as a paravector. Lorentz transformations mix the two parts, but preserve their Minkowski norm. The scalar part is not an observer-independent absolute time.</p>\n<p>For four-momentum, the invariant gives the relativistic energy–momentum relation:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\np\\bar p=\\frac{E^2}{c^2}-|\\mathbf p|^2=m^2c^2,\n\\qquad\nE^2=m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>For light, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(m=0\\)</span> and the momentum matrix is singular. This is a geometric property of a null four-vector, not a defect of its representation.</p>\n<p>Keep these distinctions in view:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The spatial algebra is Euclidean: <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_j^2=+1\\)</span>. Minkowski signature belongs to the paravector norm involving Clifford conjugation.</li>\n<li>A spatial vector, bivector, paravector, and six-vector are distinct algebraic types, even when represented by matrices of the same size.</li>\n<li>The matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(i\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span> represents the spatial pseudoscalar, not a fourth spatial basis vector.</li>\n<li>Hermitian conjugation and Clifford conjugation are different operations.</li>\n<li>The Lorentz paravector action uses <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L^\\dagger\\)</span>, not generally <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(L^{-1}\\)</span>.</li>\n<li>The Pauli representation realizes APS as an algebra; merely arranging four numbers in a generic matrix does not preserve its multiplication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Later chapters apply this toolkit to physical fields and dynamical equations.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Chapter exercises\n\n1. Multiply ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2`` and ``\\sigma_2\\sigma_1`` explicitly. Identify the corresponding bivectors in APS.\n2. Encode ``q=3+2e_1-e_3`` as a matrix. Compute its determinant, causal type, and inverse in both languages.\n3. Starting from the Pauli product identity, verify ``(\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\mathbf 1_2`` and derive the eigenvalues of a paravector matrix.\n4. For a boost along ``e_3``, use ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to derive the component formulas directly from matrix multiplication.\n5. Show that ``N=(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2`` is idempotent with trace one when ``|\\mathbf n|=1``. Explain why it also represents a future null paravector.\n\n**Checks:** In Exercise 2, the determinant is ``4``, the paravector is timelike, and its inverse is ``(3-2e_1+e_3)/4``. In Exercise 5, the corresponding paravector has temporal component ``1/2`` and spatial magnitude ``1/2``.","output":"","result":"## Chapter exercises\n\n1. Multiply ``\\sigma_1\\sigma_2`` and ``\\sigma_2\\sigma_1`` explicitly. Identify the corresponding bivectors in APS.\n2. Encode ``q=3+2e_1-e_3`` as a matrix. Compute its determinant, causal type, and inverse in both languages.\n3. Starting from the Pauli product identity, verify ``(\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\mathbf 1_2`` and derive the eigenvalues of a paravector matrix.\n4. For a boost along ``e_3``, use ``B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})`` to derive the component formulas directly from matrix multiplication.\n5. Show that ``N=(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2`` is idempotent with trace one when ``|\\mathbf n|=1``. Explain why it also represents a future null paravector.\n\n**Checks:** In Exercise 2, the determinant is ``4``, the paravector is timelike, and its inverse is ``(3-2e_1+e_3)/4``. In Exercise 5, the corresponding paravector has temporal component ``1/2`` and spatial magnitude ``1/2``.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-Physics_211_Algebra_of_Physical_Space-c5088-chapter-exercises\">\n<h1>Chapter exercises</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Multiply <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_2\\sigma_1\\)</span> explicitly. Identify the corresponding bivectors in APS.</li>\n<li>Encode <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(q=3+2e_1-e_3\\)</span> as a matrix. Compute its determinant, causal type, and inverse in both languages.</li>\n<li>Starting from the Pauli product identity, verify <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((\\mathbf p\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)^2=|\\mathbf p|^2\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span> and derive the eigenvalues of a paravector matrix.</li>\n<li>For a boost along <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(e_3\\)</span>, use <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(B=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{\\eta/2},e^{-\\eta/2})\\)</span> to derive the component formulas directly from matrix multiplication.</li>\n<li>Show that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(N=(\\mathbf 1_2+\\mathbf n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2\\)</span> is idempotent with trace one when <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(|\\mathbf n|=1\\)</span>. Explain why it also represents a future null paravector.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Checks:</strong> In Exercise 2, the determinant is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(4\\)</span>, the paravector is timelike, and its inverse is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((3-2e_1+e_3)/4\\)</span>. In Exercise 5, the corresponding paravector has temporal component <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1/2\\)</span> and spatial magnitude <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1/2\\)</span>.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"Adjoint representation\n:   The action of a group on its Lie algebra by conjugation, ``F\\mapsto\\ell F\\ell^{-1}``; here “adjoint” does not mean Hermitian adjoint or matrix adjugate.\n\nAdjugate\n:   For a two-by-two matrix ``M``, the matrix ``\\operatorname{adj}M`` satisfying ``M\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)\\mathbf 1_2``; unlike the Hermitian adjoint, it does not conjugate entries.\n\nAlgebra of physical space (APS)\n:   The real Clifford algebra ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` of three-dimensional Euclidean space, used here to represent spatial geometry and relativistic paravectors.\n\nAnticommutator\n:   The sum ``AC+CA`` of the two possible product orders.\n\nBiparavector\n:   The antisymmetric product ``\\mathcal B(p,q)=\\tfrac12(p\\bar q-q\\bar p)`` of two real paravectors; it is a simple spacetime bivector represented in APS as a six-vector.\n\nBivector\n:   A grade-two Clifford element representing an oriented plane; in three spatial dimensions it is dual to a vector by multiplication with the pseudoscalar.\n\nBoost\n:   A Lorentz transformation mixing temporal and spatial components along a specified direction; its parameter ``\\eta`` is the rapidity.\n\nCausal type\n:   The classification of a real paravector as timelike, null, or spacelike according as its Minkowski norm is positive, zero, or negative.\n\nClifford algebra\n:   An associative algebra generated by a vector space with a product encoding its quadratic form; here the spatial algebra is ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}``.\n\nClifford conjugation\n:   The order-reversing APS operation that changes the sign of each spatial vector basis element; on a paravector ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` it gives ``\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p``.\n\nCommutator\n:   The Lie bracket ``[A,C]=AC-CA`` induced by an associative product.\n\nComplex vector\n:   A linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in the central complex field ``\\mathbb C_I``; its real and imaginary parts encode vector and bivector grades.\n\nDirac spinor\n:   A relativistic spinor combining the two opposite-chirality Weyl-spinor representations.\n\nEven subalgebra\n:   The APS subalgebra consisting of scalar and bivector grades; in ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` it is isomorphic to the quaternions.\n\nGeometric product\n:   The associative Clifford product combining the inner and outer products; for spatial vectors ``\\mathbf a`` and ``\\mathbf b`` it is ``\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b``.\n\nGrade\n:   The number of vector factors in a homogeneous Clifford element: scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar have grades zero through three.\n\nGrade involution\n:   The automorphism that multiplies each grade-``r`` element by ``(-1)^r``.\n\nHermitian adjoint\n:   The complex-conjugate transpose of a matrix, denoted ``M^\\dagger``; in the Pauli realization of APS it represents reversion.\n\nIdempotent\n:   An element ``f`` satisfying ``f^2=f``; the APS elements ``(1\\pm\\mathbf n)/2`` are examples for a unit vector ``\\mathbf n``.\n\nLeft ideal\n:   A linear subspace of an algebra closed under multiplication on the left by arbitrary algebra elements; ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f`` is a spinor module when ``f`` is a suitable primitive idempotent.\n\nLie algebra\n:   A vector space equipped with a bilinear, antisymmetric bracket satisfying the Jacobi identity; the APS commutator supplies such a bracket.\n\nLorentz transformation\n:   A linear transformation of spacetime preserving the Minkowski inner product; the sandwich action by ``\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` represents the proper orthochronous subgroup.\n\nLorentz spin group\n:   The group ``\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` acting on two-component spinors and, by a sandwich action, inducing proper orthochronous Lorentz transformations on paravectors.\n\nMinkowski norm\n:   The quadratic form ``p\\bar p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2`` on real paravectors, with signature ``(+,-,-,-)``.\n\nMinimal left ideal\n:   A nonzero left ideal containing no smaller nonzero left ideal; in this chapter it realizes a two-component spinor representation.\n\nMultivector\n:   A general element of the Clifford algebra, formed as a sum of scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar parts.\n\nNilpotent\n:   An element ``N`` for which some positive integer power vanishes; in the example ``N=e_1+Ie_2``, one has ``N^2=0``.\n\nNull paravector\n:   A nonzero real paravector with zero Minkowski norm; its Pauli matrix is singular and rank one when future-directed.\n\nPauli matrices\n:   The three Hermitian matrices ``\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3`` satisfying the spatial Clifford multiplication relations and representing the spatial basis vectors of APS.\n\nPauli representation\n:   The algebra isomorphism ``\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\to M_2(\\mathbb C)`` sending the spatial basis vectors to the Pauli matrices.\n\nParavector\n:   An element ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` in the scalar-plus-spatial-vector subspace of APS, representing a spacetime four-vector with a Minkowski norm.\n\nPseudoscalar\n:   The highest-grade element ``I=e_1e_2e_3`` of APS; it is central, squares to ``-1``, and provides the algebra's geometric imaginary unit.\n\nQuaternion\n:   An element of the even APS subalgebra, which is isomorphic to the real quaternion algebra with basis ``1,q_1,q_2,q_3``.\n\nReversion\n:   The order-reversing APS operation that fixes each spatial vector basis element; under the Pauli representation it corresponds to Hermitian adjoint.\n\nRotor\n:   A unit even APS element that acts on spatial vectors by ``\\mathbf v\\mapsto R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger`` to represent a rotation.\n\nSandwich action\n:   The transformation ``p\\mapsto\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger`` of a paravector by a Lorentz spin representative; it preserves the paravector's Minkowski norm.\n\nSix-vector\n:   An APS element ``F=\\mathbf a+I\\mathbf b`` containing vector and bivector grades, with six real components; it transforms by similarity under Lorentz spin transformations.\n\nSpacelike\n:   Having negative Minkowski norm.\n\nSpacetime bivector\n:   An antisymmetric rank-two object in four-dimensional spacetime; its six independent components are represented by APS six-vectors.\n\nSpectral decomposition\n:   The expression of a paravector as a sum of orthogonal idempotents weighted by its eigenvalues, ``p=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-``.\n\nSpinor\n:   An element of a representation space whose defining feature is its transformation law; in this chapter the main example is a two-component Weyl spinor transforming as ``\\psi\\mapsto L\\psi``.\n\nTimelike\n:   Having positive Minkowski norm; a nonzero timelike paravector has two nonzero eigenvalues of the same sign.\n\nWedge product\n:   The antisymmetric part ``\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\tfrac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a)`` of the geometric product, representing an oriented area element.\n\nWeyl spinor\n:   A two-component spinor carrying one chiral representation of the Lorentz spin group; its opposite chirality transforms by ``(L^\\dagger)^{-1}``.\n\nZero divisor\n:   A nonzero algebra element that annihilates a nonzero element on at least one side; in APS these correspond to singular two-by-two complex matrices.","output":"","result":"Adjoint representation\n:   The action of a group on its Lie algebra by conjugation, ``F\\mapsto\\ell F\\ell^{-1}``; here “adjoint” does not mean Hermitian adjoint or matrix adjugate.\n\nAdjugate\n:   For a two-by-two matrix ``M``, the matrix ``\\operatorname{adj}M`` satisfying ``M\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)\\mathbf 1_2``; unlike the Hermitian adjoint, it does not conjugate entries.\n\nAlgebra of physical space (APS)\n:   The real Clifford algebra ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` of three-dimensional Euclidean space, used here to represent spatial geometry and relativistic paravectors.\n\nAnticommutator\n:   The sum ``AC+CA`` of the two possible product orders.\n\nBiparavector\n:   The antisymmetric product ``\\mathcal B(p,q)=\\tfrac12(p\\bar q-q\\bar p)`` of two real paravectors; it is a simple spacetime bivector represented in APS as a six-vector.\n\nBivector\n:   A grade-two Clifford element representing an oriented plane; in three spatial dimensions it is dual to a vector by multiplication with the pseudoscalar.\n\nBoost\n:   A Lorentz transformation mixing temporal and spatial components along a specified direction; its parameter ``\\eta`` is the rapidity.\n\nCausal type\n:   The classification of a real paravector as timelike, null, or spacelike according as its Minkowski norm is positive, zero, or negative.\n\nClifford algebra\n:   An associative algebra generated by a vector space with a product encoding its quadratic form; here the spatial algebra is ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}``.\n\nClifford conjugation\n:   The order-reversing APS operation that changes the sign of each spatial vector basis element; on a paravector ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` it gives ``\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p``.\n\nCommutator\n:   The Lie bracket ``[A,C]=AC-CA`` induced by an associative product.\n\nComplex vector\n:   A linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in the central complex field ``\\mathbb C_I``; its real and imaginary parts encode vector and bivector grades.\n\nDirac spinor\n:   A relativistic spinor combining the two opposite-chirality Weyl-spinor representations.\n\nEven subalgebra\n:   The APS subalgebra consisting of scalar and bivector grades; in ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}`` it is isomorphic to the quaternions.\n\nGeometric product\n:   The associative Clifford product combining the inner and outer products; for spatial vectors ``\\mathbf a`` and ``\\mathbf b`` it is ``\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b``.\n\nGrade\n:   The number of vector factors in a homogeneous Clifford element: scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar have grades zero through three.\n\nGrade involution\n:   The automorphism that multiplies each grade-``r`` element by ``(-1)^r``.\n\nHermitian adjoint\n:   The complex-conjugate transpose of a matrix, denoted ``M^\\dagger``; in the Pauli realization of APS it represents reversion.\n\nIdempotent\n:   An element ``f`` satisfying ``f^2=f``; the APS elements ``(1\\pm\\mathbf n)/2`` are examples for a unit vector ``\\mathbf n``.\n\nLeft ideal\n:   A linear subspace of an algebra closed under multiplication on the left by arbitrary algebra elements; ``\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f`` is a spinor module when ``f`` is a suitable primitive idempotent.\n\nLie algebra\n:   A vector space equipped with a bilinear, antisymmetric bracket satisfying the Jacobi identity; the APS commutator supplies such a bracket.\n\nLorentz transformation\n:   A linear transformation of spacetime preserving the Minkowski inner product; the sandwich action by ``\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` represents the proper orthochronous subgroup.\n\nLorentz spin group\n:   The group ``\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)`` acting on two-component spinors and, by a sandwich action, inducing proper orthochronous Lorentz transformations on paravectors.\n\nMinkowski norm\n:   The quadratic form ``p\\bar p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2`` on real paravectors, with signature ``(+,-,-,-)``.\n\nMinimal left ideal\n:   A nonzero left ideal containing no smaller nonzero left ideal; in this chapter it realizes a two-component spinor representation.\n\nMultivector\n:   A general element of the Clifford algebra, formed as a sum of scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar parts.\n\nNilpotent\n:   An element ``N`` for which some positive integer power vanishes; in the example ``N=e_1+Ie_2``, one has ``N^2=0``.\n\nNull paravector\n:   A nonzero real paravector with zero Minkowski norm; its Pauli matrix is singular and rank one when future-directed.\n\nPauli matrices\n:   The three Hermitian matrices ``\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3`` satisfying the spatial Clifford multiplication relations and representing the spatial basis vectors of APS.\n\nPauli representation\n:   The algebra isomorphism ``\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\to M_2(\\mathbb C)`` sending the spatial basis vectors to the Pauli matrices.\n\nParavector\n:   An element ``p=p^0+\\mathbf p`` in the scalar-plus-spatial-vector subspace of APS, representing a spacetime four-vector with a Minkowski norm.\n\nPseudoscalar\n:   The highest-grade element ``I=e_1e_2e_3`` of APS; it is central, squares to ``-1``, and provides the algebra's geometric imaginary unit.\n\nQuaternion\n:   An element of the even APS subalgebra, which is isomorphic to the real quaternion algebra with basis ``1,q_1,q_2,q_3``.\n\nReversion\n:   The order-reversing APS operation that fixes each spatial vector basis element; under the Pauli representation it corresponds to Hermitian adjoint.\n\nRotor\n:   A unit even APS element that acts on spatial vectors by ``\\mathbf v\\mapsto R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger`` to represent a rotation.\n\nSandwich action\n:   The transformation ``p\\mapsto\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger`` of a paravector by a Lorentz spin representative; it preserves the paravector's Minkowski norm.\n\nSix-vector\n:   An APS element ``F=\\mathbf a+I\\mathbf b`` containing vector and bivector grades, with six real components; it transforms by similarity under Lorentz spin transformations.\n\nSpacelike\n:   Having negative Minkowski norm.\n\nSpacetime bivector\n:   An antisymmetric rank-two object in four-dimensional spacetime; its six independent components are represented by APS six-vectors.\n\nSpectral decomposition\n:   The expression of a paravector as a sum of orthogonal idempotents weighted by its eigenvalues, ``p=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-``.\n\nSpinor\n:   An element of a representation space whose defining feature is its transformation law; in this chapter the main example is a two-component Weyl spinor transforming as ``\\psi\\mapsto L\\psi``.\n\nTimelike\n:   Having positive Minkowski norm; a nonzero timelike paravector has two nonzero eigenvalues of the same sign.\n\nWedge product\n:   The antisymmetric part ``\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\tfrac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a)`` of the geometric product, representing an oriented area element.\n\nWeyl spinor\n:   A two-component spinor carrying one chiral representation of the Lorentz spin group; its opposite chirality transforms by ``(L^\\dagger)^{-1}``.\n\nZero divisor\n:   A nonzero algebra element that annihilates a nonzero element on at least one side; in APS these correspond to singular two-by-two complex matrices.","resultHTML":"<dl>\n<dt>Adjoint representation</dt>\n<dd>The action of a group on its Lie algebra by conjugation, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F\\mapsto\\ell F\\ell^{-1}\\)</span>; here “adjoint” does not mean Hermitian adjoint or matrix adjugate.</dd>\n<dt>Adjugate</dt>\n<dd>For a two-by-two matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\)</span>, the matrix <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\operatorname{adj}M\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M\\operatorname{adj}M=(\\det M)\\mathbf 1_2\\)</span>; unlike the Hermitian adjoint, it does not conjugate entries.</dd>\n<dt>Algebra of physical space (APS)</dt>\n<dd>The real Clifford algebra <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span> of three-dimensional Euclidean space, used here to represent spatial geometry and relativistic paravectors.</dd>\n<dt>Anticommutator</dt>\n<dd>The sum <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(AC+CA\\)</span> of the two possible product orders.</dd>\n<dt>Biparavector</dt>\n<dd>The antisymmetric product <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathcal B(p,q)=\\tfrac12(p\\bar q-q\\bar p)\\)</span> of two real paravectors; it is a simple spacetime bivector represented in APS as a six-vector.</dd>\n<dt>Bivector</dt>\n<dd>A grade-two Clifford element representing an oriented plane; in three spatial dimensions it is dual to a vector by multiplication with the pseudoscalar.</dd>\n<dt>Boost</dt>\n<dd>A Lorentz transformation mixing temporal and spatial components along a specified direction; its parameter <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\eta\\)</span> is the rapidity.</dd>\n<dt>Causal type</dt>\n<dd>The classification of a real paravector as timelike, null, or spacelike according as its Minkowski norm is positive, zero, or negative.</dd>\n<dt>Clifford algebra</dt>\n<dd>An associative algebra generated by a vector space with a product encoding its quadratic form; here the spatial algebra is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Clifford conjugation</dt>\n<dd>The order-reversing APS operation that changes the sign of each spatial vector basis element; on a paravector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=p^0+\\mathbf p\\)</span> it gives <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\bar p=p^0-\\mathbf p\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Commutator</dt>\n<dd>The Lie bracket <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\([A,C]=AC-CA\\)</span> induced by an associative product.</dd>\n<dt>Complex vector</dt>\n<dd>A linear combination of the spatial basis vectors with coefficients in the central complex field <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbb C_I\\)</span>; its real and imaginary parts encode vector and bivector grades.</dd>\n<dt>Dirac spinor</dt>\n<dd>A relativistic spinor combining the two opposite-chirality Weyl-spinor representations.</dd>\n<dt>Even subalgebra</dt>\n<dd>The APS subalgebra consisting of scalar and bivector grades; in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\)</span> it is isomorphic to the quaternions.</dd>\n<dt>Geometric product</dt>\n<dd>The associative Clifford product combining the inner and outer products; for spatial vectors <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf b\\)</span> it is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf b+\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Grade</dt>\n<dd>The number of vector factors in a homogeneous Clifford element: scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar have grades zero through three.</dd>\n<dt>Grade involution</dt>\n<dd>The automorphism that multiplies each grade-<span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> element by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((-1)^r\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Hermitian adjoint</dt>\n<dd>The complex-conjugate transpose of a matrix, denoted <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(M^\\dagger\\)</span>; in the Pauli realization of APS it represents reversion.</dd>\n<dt>Idempotent</dt>\n<dd>An element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^2=f\\)</span>; the APS elements <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((1\\pm\\mathbf n)/2\\)</span> are examples for a unit vector <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf n\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Left ideal</dt>\n<dd>A linear subspace of an algebra closed under multiplication on the left by arbitrary algebra elements; <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}f\\)</span> is a spinor module when <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is a suitable primitive idempotent.</dd>\n<dt>Lie algebra</dt>\n<dd>A vector space equipped with a bilinear, antisymmetric bracket satisfying the Jacobi identity; the APS commutator supplies such a bracket.</dd>\n<dt>Lorentz transformation</dt>\n<dd>A linear transformation of spacetime preserving the Minkowski inner product; the sandwich action by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span> represents the proper orthochronous subgroup.</dd>\n<dt>Lorentz spin group</dt>\n<dd>The group <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathrm{SL}(2,\\mathbb C)\\)</span> acting on two-component spinors and, by a sandwich action, inducing proper orthochronous Lorentz transformations on paravectors.</dd>\n<dt>Minkowski norm</dt>\n<dd>The quadratic form <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p\\bar p=(p^0)^2-|\\mathbf p|^2\\)</span> on real paravectors, with signature <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((+,-,-,-)\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Minimal left ideal</dt>\n<dd>A nonzero left ideal containing no smaller nonzero left ideal; in this chapter it realizes a two-component spinor representation.</dd>\n<dt>Multivector</dt>\n<dd>A general element of the Clifford algebra, formed as a sum of scalar, vector, bivector, and pseudoscalar parts.</dd>\n<dt>Nilpotent</dt>\n<dd>An element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(N\\)</span> for which some positive integer power vanishes; in the example <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(N=e_1+Ie_2\\)</span>, one has <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(N^2=0\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Null paravector</dt>\n<dd>A nonzero real paravector with zero Minkowski norm; its Pauli matrix is singular and rank one when future-directed.</dd>\n<dt>Pauli matrices</dt>\n<dd>The three Hermitian matrices <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3\\)</span> satisfying the spatial Clifford multiplication relations and representing the spatial basis vectors of APS.</dd>\n<dt>Pauli representation</dt>\n<dd>The algebra isomorphism <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\rho:\\mathrm{Cl}_{3,0}\\to M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)</span> sending the spatial basis vectors to the Pauli matrices.</dd>\n<dt>Paravector</dt>\n<dd>An element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=p^0+\\mathbf p\\)</span> in the scalar-plus-spatial-vector subspace of APS, representing a spacetime four-vector with a Minkowski norm.</dd>\n<dt>Pseudoscalar</dt>\n<dd>The highest-grade element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I=e_1e_2e_3\\)</span> of APS; it is central, squares to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(-1\\)</span>, and provides the algebra’s geometric imaginary unit.</dd>\n<dt>Quaternion</dt>\n<dd>An element of the even APS subalgebra, which is isomorphic to the real quaternion algebra with basis <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(1,q_1,q_2,q_3\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Reversion</dt>\n<dd>The order-reversing APS operation that fixes each spatial vector basis element; under the Pauli representation it corresponds to Hermitian adjoint.</dd>\n<dt>Rotor</dt>\n<dd>A unit even APS element that acts on spatial vectors by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf v\\mapsto R\\mathbf vR^\\dagger\\)</span> to represent a rotation.</dd>\n<dt>Sandwich action</dt>\n<dd>The transformation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p\\mapsto\\ell p\\ell^\\dagger\\)</span> of a paravector by a Lorentz spin representative; it preserves the paravector’s Minkowski norm.</dd>\n<dt>Six-vector</dt>\n<dd>An APS element <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(F=\\mathbf a+I\\mathbf b\\)</span> containing vector and bivector grades, with six real components; it transforms by similarity under Lorentz spin transformations.</dd>\n<dt>Spacelike</dt>\n<dd>Having negative Minkowski norm.</dd>\n<dt>Spacetime bivector</dt>\n<dd>An antisymmetric rank-two object in four-dimensional spacetime; its six independent components are represented by APS six-vectors.</dd>\n<dt>Spectral decomposition</dt>\n<dd>The expression of a paravector as a sum of orthogonal idempotents weighted by its eigenvalues, <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(p=(p^0+r)f_++(p^0-r)f_-\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Spinor</dt>\n<dd>An element of a representation space whose defining feature is its transformation law; in this chapter the main example is a two-component Weyl spinor transforming as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\psi\\mapsto L\\psi\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Timelike</dt>\n<dd>Having positive Minkowski norm; a nonzero timelike paravector has two nonzero eigenvalues of the same sign.</dd>\n<dt>Wedge product</dt>\n<dd>The antisymmetric part <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\mathbf a\\wedge\\mathbf b=\\tfrac12(\\mathbf a\\mathbf b-\\mathbf b\\mathbf a)\\)</span> of the geometric product, representing an oriented area element.</dd>\n<dt>Weyl spinor</dt>\n<dd>A two-component spinor carrying one chiral representation of the Lorentz spin group; its opposite chirality transforms by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\((L^\\dagger)^{-1}\\)</span>.</dd>\n<dt>Zero divisor</dt>\n<dd>A nonzero algebra element that annihilates a nonzero element on at least one side; in APS these correspond to singular two-by-two complex matrices.</dd>\n</dl>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]